| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Core link and routines | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 3 | Think, feel, do. *What can I do between a big feeling and what I do next?* | 11 | g3-rds-careful-thinking; g3-rds-good-for-us; g3-sa-feel-think-link; g3-sa-name-feelings; g3-sm-big-feelings; g3-sm-calm-down |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e3-character-actions, how story characters' feelings and motivations drive their actions | Three groups. Group 1, spot it and name it: the feelings faces chart gains a 1 to 5 strength scale and the routine "spot the body clue, name it, rate it", used on school-day scene cards (a lost recess game, a hard spelling quiz), with the reason that naming a feeling first makes it easier to handle (SA3); a think-feel-do chain for a character whose classmate did not wave back, then "catch the thought, test it, try another" and a second thought that fits the facts (SA5). Group 2, calm it, taught calm-down first: the calm-down steps grow to four, stop, breathe, name it, choose, on a plan card with three body tools (slow breaths, a stretch, counting back from ten) for two school moments, rehearsed daily for a week (the carry-out cue) with a reason for each step (SM2); the four steps before acting when a teammate grabs the ball, and the difference between reacting and responding in a sentence (SM1). Group 3, choose with care: stop-think-choose grows to four steps, stop, list two or three choices, think what each would cause, choose and check; two invented classmates doing the same task, one who rushed and one who used the steps, and one place at home the steps would help (RD5); two options for a recess choice (finish a game indoors, or play with a classmate who is alone) weighed for one's own well-being and others', and a pick with a reason (RD6). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 14 and 18: the calm-down tool choice, hidden feelings, careful thinking) in the openings; unit 1 opens the band, so where the class did not have SEL K to 2 the first Understanding session teaches the grade 2 card forms first (Leave-out). End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (a character's feeling, its clue and strength; a second thought that fits the facts; the step a character skipped) and the four calm-down steps rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: "stop, breathe, name it, choose" before transitions. Family activity: practice the plan card's three body tools at home when calm. Tools: the feelings faces chart with its scale, the calm-down plan card, the four-step stop-think-choose card, the think-feel-do chain sheet, scene cards. Curio Class writes the chart scale, both cards, the chain sheet, the scene cards, the page and the family note. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Out: a student's own feelings in a check (never asked); two characters' chains (unit 10). Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Self-Awareness.3, CASEL.Self-Awareness.5, CASEL.Self-Management.1, CASEL.Self-Management.2, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6. | First because it grows three cards the year uses: the strength scale (unit 3), the calm-down steps (every opening) and stop-think-choose (units 2, 3 and 5). It runs beside ELA grade 3 unit 1, where characters' feelings and actions are read. Six sub-skills, three of them new card forms, so three groups and 11 sessions (one of the year's four 11s). Health grade 3 unit 4 (weeks 17 to 21) uses these feelings words later (Cross-subject). Sessions 1 to 11 of the year, weeks 1 to 6. | Candidate: the plan card's three body tools checked against health 3 to 5's calm tools so one card serves both; the "test it" step's look-for written so it never asks a student to test their own thought aloud | No clinical words; "catch the thought, test it" is a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment, and no student tests their own thoughts aloud. Feelings are named for characters in checks. Body tools are plain body skills with no spiritual framing; mental health topics are health's. Stretches standing beside desks with room to reach. Disclosure: health's steps in every unit (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something": the school's reporting route and the counselor). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (body tools). |
| 2 | 3 | Who I am and what I bring. *What makes up who a person is, and what does each person bring to a class?* | 8 | g3-sa-my-strengths; g3-sa-tell-truth; g3-sa-what-interests-me; g3-sa-who-i-am; g3-soc-notice-good |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:ss3-biography, the achievements and contributions people make | Two groups. The private interest log starts on session 1 and runs two weeks (the carry-out cue): what the student chose to do in free time, and why. Group 1, who I am: an identity web for an invented character (a team, a helper role at home, a club, a language) in three steps, list the groups, say what each adds, say where each part shows, and why knowing it helps; a private web of their own if they choose (SA1); a strengths hunt in three steps, recall a task that went well, name the skill it used, find another place it helps, for a story character first, a language spoken counting as a strength, each backed by an example (SA2). Group 2, what I stand for and bring: the honest action on a school-day case (a classmate's answer sheet left in view) chosen with stop-think-choose, the value named, and what honesty cost and kept (SA4); one entry picked from the private log and a purpose named for it (SA9); a three-part shout-out after a planned partner task, what they did, how, why it mattered, and a vague "good job" rewritten (SO2). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 13, 14 and 17) and unit 1's stop-think-choose in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year course-level check after this unit is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks): an unseen page (the honest action and its value; a strength with its example; a shout-out rewritten) and a shout-out rehearsed with a partner from an unseen task card. Carry it out: three-part shout-outs at the end of group work. Family activity: build an identity web for a made-up character together, or a real one if the family chooses. Tools: the web sheet, the strengths hunt card, the private log, stop-think-choose, task cards. Curio Class writes the model webs, the case, the log page and the family note. Provisional cost for 30: about $9 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Self-Awareness.1, CASEL.Self-Awareness.2, CASEL.Self-Awareness.4, CASEL.Self-Awareness.9, CASEL.Social Awareness.2. | After unit 1 because the honest choice uses the four-step stop-think-choose; before unit 3 because a student names their own groups and strengths before reading others'. Five sub-skills, mostly reflection and talk, so two groups and 10 sessions, its Check and Respond being the mid-year check. Social studies grade 3 unit 7 (biographies, course days 141 to 160) comes later in the year (Cross-subject). Sessions 12 to 19 of the year, weeks 6 to 10; the mid-year check is sessions 20 and 21. | Candidate: the two-week log's "steps followed" look-for written on the class sheet; the model webs checked for range (families of every kind, a home language, a helper role) | Invented character first; a student may make up their own web; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check. Home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about. SA4 is checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct. The log and private web stay with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity (the agent first studies an education association's published guidance on families and cultures in the classroom). |
| 3 | 3 | Walking in their shoes. *How can I tell what someone else feels and sees, and what is the kind thing to do?* | 11 | g3-rel-different-ways; g3-sa-snap-judgments; g3-soc-care-when-struggle; g3-soc-read-feelings; g3-soc-thankful; g3-soc-their-view; g3-soc-why-rules |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e3-point-of-view, telling one's own view apart from a character's | Three groups. The class gratitude wall starts on session 1 and runs two weeks (the carry-out cue). Group 1, reading others: three kinds of clue (face, body, voice) read in short teacher-acted school scenes, the strength rated on the feelings faces scale, the guess checked with a question (SO4); the "shoes switch" on a school-day scene card, what each person saw, wants and why each acted, then the scene retold from the other side (SO1); three care steps, notice, ask what would help, do it, acted with a partner for a character who lost a library book, and why asking first beats guessing (SO3). Group 2, checking my guess: a three-step check on a snap judgment (a quiet new student called stuck-up), catch the first thought, ask what I actually know, find out before deciding, with stop-think-choose, and why first guesses mislead (SA6); with an invented story partner from another place on a shared class task, three steps, notice a different way, ask about it with respect, try it or combine it; the partner's way is a way of keeping score or taking turns in a game from home, the place and the way taken by the unit plan from a cited source (RS3). Group 3, thanks and systems: each gratitude note names the act, the person and the difference it made, and why noticing good things changes a class (SO5); one school system (the lunch line) followed in three steps, the rule, what it makes people do, what would happen without it (SO8). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 13, 15, 16 and 18) and unit 1's strength scale and stop-think-choose in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (three clues and a checking question; the other side's reason; the snap-judgment check) and the care steps rehearsed from an unseen card, the teacher playing the one who struggles. Carry it out: "ask, don't guess" in group work. Family activity: retell a family story from another person's side, real or made up. Tools: the feelings faces chart, the shoes switch card, the care steps card, the gratitude wall, scene cards, the story partner page. Curio Class writes the scenes, the story partner page from its source, the cards and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.3, CASEL.Self-Awareness.6, CASEL.Social Awareness.1, CASEL.Social Awareness.3, CASEL.Social Awareness.4, CASEL.Social Awareness.5, CASEL.Social Awareness.8. | After unit 1 because reading others uses its strength scale and the snap-judgment check uses its stop-think-choose; after unit 2 because a student reads others after naming their own groups. Seven sub-skills, so three groups and 11 sessions. ELA grade 3 unit 3 (point of view, course days 81 to 119) comes after; social studies grade 3 unit 2 (rules and rights, course days 25 to 48) comes before (Cross-subject). Sessions 22 to 32 of the year, weeks 11 to 16. | Candidate: the teacher-acted scenes scripted in the unit plan; the story partner's game way taken from a source the sensitivity agent has read, not one of PE's or arts' grades 3 to 5 picks | SA6 on invented cases, the wrong guess resting on a neutral trait (quiet, new), never on skin, hair, dress, faith or accent; never blame of a group. RS3: no student is asked to explain their own culture; the partner's way is one invented child's, from a cited source. If a student tells of harm or risk in a care scene, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity (the agent first studies the source for the partner's way). |
