23: ## The pass rule
24: 
25: **Accepted for now (Cory, 2026-11-11), to be tested on the first finished course.** No course has been read
26: against it yet, so it may prove too hard or too easy.
27: It copies the shape of the instruments read: alignment, accuracy and feasibility are a door that stops a review, and
28: the rest is scored.
29: 
30: - **The door: C1, C2, C3 and C14, each passed in full.** A course that fails one is not read further until fixed.
31: - **C4 to C13, C15, C16:** each is marked pass, part pass or fail. A finished course has no fail and no more than
32:   three part passes.
33: - **Pass:** no "fails if" condition is found in the sample. **Part pass:** a condition is found in one sampled unit
34:   only, and the fix is local. **Fail:** it is found in two or more units, or is built into the course's design.
35: - **The sample.** The reviewer reads the whole scope and sequence and coverage table, then three whole units picked by the
36:   reviewer, never by the builder, one from each third of the year: every lesson, page, tool and check in them. A
37:   course of four units or fewer is read whole. Below, "three units" means this sample.
38: - The subject part is read the same way: no criterion failed, and the part pass count above covers core and subject
39:   criteria together.
40: - **A small course is read the same way.** SEL and library skills courses may be smaller than other courses by
41:   the nature of the subject (decided 2026-11-11). They are "held to the whole core at the size its scope and sequence states":
42:   every criterion from C1 to C16 and every criterion of the subject part still applies, and what is measured is
43:   the number of units and days the scope and sequence itself sets out, not the size of a math or science course. Being small
44:   waives no criterion. **Smaller means fewer minutes a week, never fewer units** (Cory 2026-11-15, later.csv L43): each
45:   SEL and library course runs the whole year at one or two sessions a week, or about one every two weeks in the two library band courses (its frame states which), so every standard
46:   of its framework is still taught, practised spaced out over the year and checked. This fits how schools staff these
47:   subjects (a counselor or librarian seeing a class once a week).
48: - **Beyond the three units,** the reviewer reads: for C1, the whole coverage table, tracing ten standards of the
49:   reviewer's choice to lesson and check item; for C4, the progression page; for C8 and C9, one cumulative check;
50:   for C13, the approach page; for C3 and C16, all public copy for the course and the logic model.
51: 
52: ## The shared core
53: 
54: Layers are those in docs/ROADMAP.md, "The layers": 1 foundation, 2 standards, 3 approach, 4 progressions, 5 scope and sequence,
55: 6 unit plan and section plan, 7 lessons, tools, print and checks. The rows recur across the instruments read in full (see
56: the sources table). No count per row is given, because the first draft's "Seen in" column was not reliable.
57: 
58: | # | A reviewer looks for | What shows it in a Curio Class course | Layer | Fails if |
59: |---|---|---|---|---|
60: | C1 | **Every standard of the course is named and taught in full,** at the depth its wording asks, with little time on content from outside the course | The scope and sequence's standards table with days; a coverage table pointing each standard to lesson, tool and check item; a named edition loaded in data/standards/ | 2, 5 | Any standard lacks a lesson, practice or a check item. A traced standard is taught below its wording. More than 10 percent of teaching days go to content outside the course's standards (our number, reset per subject once its scope and sequence form is known). The edition is not named or not loaded |
61: | C2 | **A year plan with reasons:** units in order, why, days per unit, minutes per lesson, a shorter path | The scope and sequence: every teaching day, the reason for the order, the stated year and period length, the short path, gap days | 5 | A teaching day has no plan. The order has no stated reason. No minutes per lesson. No short path. The days do not add up to the stated year |
62: | C3 | **Nothing wrong, nothing sold, authors plain.** (a) Facts, figures, terms and pictures are correct and current, with sources. (b) No product, brand or provider is promoted; opinion is marked. (c) Who wrote it, and how, is stated | The accuracy read at the Finish check, with the reader named; a source line on each fact page; public copy that names no companies and says how the course was made. Health: see its criterion 7 | 7 | (a) An uncorrected error in a key, a definition or a figure; a dated figure with no date or source. (b) Any promotion. (c) No statement of authorship |
63: | C4 | **Each idea rests on the last:** what earlier grades taught, how units link, what comes next | The subject's progression page; the scope and sequence's builds-on and comes-back columns; the readiness check or opening retrieval task on each unit plan | 4, 5, 6 | A unit leans on an idea nothing earlier taught and no readiness check or retrieval task catches it. No progression page. A band course does not say what the band before taught |
64: | C5 | **A unit overview that prepares the teacher:** purpose, background knowledge, the unit's words, how it is assessed | The section plan's fields; the teacher page that teaches the teacher | 6, 7 | The teacher page restates the lessons and adds no background. No word list. Assessment not stated |
65: | C6 | **Lessons ready to teach:** objective, questions, tasks, materials, time per part, grouping | Daily plans and student pages for every scope and sequence day; the supplies list; the projector path on tool days | 7 | A scope and sequence day has no plan or no student page. A lesson lacks an objective, materials or times. The teacher must write the questions or tasks |
66: | C7 | **New things are taught, then practised with fading help,** spread over time, and they come back | The section plan field naming who holds the model each day and the day it is withdrawn; the plan for what earlier work returns | 3, 6 | A new skill is first met in independent work. No day is named for withdrawing the model. Nothing from earlier units returns |
67: | C8 | **An assessment system:** checks before, during and after teaching, each tied to named standards, more than one kind of task, doing where the subject is a doing subject, and students judging and tracking their own work | The unaided check in each tool, readiness checks and end-of-unit assessments on each unit plan (where a unit assumes only rows used the year before or earlier the same year, its opening retrieval task's teacher notes, named in the unit plan, are the before-teaching evidence), cumulative checks on the scope and sequence; a student self-check page per unit | 6, 7 | One of the three sizes is missing. An item names no standard. A doing subject is checked by recall alone. Students never judge or track their own work in a unit |
68: | C9 | **The teacher can score it and act on it:** keys, rubrics, scored samples, what each wrong answer shows, what to do next | Keys that explain each wrong answer and the reteach; a rubric and sample pieces for every open task; the respond day; the class record sheet | 6, 7 | A key gives answers only. An open task has no rubric, or samples at fewer than two levels. No next step. No record sheet |
69: | C10 | **Every learner works on the course's content:** help for students behind that keeps the goal, deeper extensions, guidance for disabilities, a reading load that fits the grade with help for students who read below it | The three hooks on every section plan; the reteach per readiness gap; extension on the same standard; the section plan's reading load field | 3, 6, 7 | A support lowers the goal or swaps the content. No extension on the same standard. No guidance for disabilities. Reading load field empty, or pages above grade with no help |
70: | C11 | **English learners are supported inside the lesson:** the language the lesson asks for, key words in depth, frames, levels where the subject can, and help telling a language gap from a content gap | The English learner hook in every tool and teacher page; Word Wall cards; a line in each key on answers that show a language gap | 6, 7 | Supports are a general list not tied to the lesson. No frames. Nothing helps the teacher tell a language gap from a content gap |
71: | C12 | **People are shown with care and range:** many voices and places, no stereotypes, no shaming, hard facts told plainly | The fresh-agent sensitivity read (FOUNDATION promise 7); a record per unit of whose work and voices appear | 1, 7 | A stereotype or shaming passes. One group carries all the examples. A hard fact is softened until it is false. No read on record |
72: | C13 | **The approach is explained, and families are told** | The subject's approach page, with sources and how much of each was read; a family note per unit, in English and Spanish, written when the unit is finished | 3, 7 | No approach page. A research claim rests on a source nobody read. A sampled unit has no family note |
73: | C14 | **It can be run in an ordinary room:** time, space, supplies, cost and teacher knowledge are stated and modest | The supplies list with cheap stand-ins; the paper path for every tool day; time per lesson; a free site | 1, 5, 7 | The course needs space, kit, time or training most schools lack and gives no stand-in. A cost to the school is not stated. A tool day has no paper path |
74: | C15 | **Screens serve the learning:** tied to the objective, run on school devices, usable without mouse or colour, private, with a non-screen path | The tool's named job in the section plan; the access check; single-file offline tools that store nothing off the device | 6, 7 | A tool has no named job. It fails keyboard-only or colour-free play. It needs an install, an account or a network. It stores data off the device |
75: | C16 | **Honest about evidence** | A one-page logic model per course; the plain statement that the course has not been tried with children | 1, 3 | Any claim of effect. No logic model. Research cited that nobody on the project read |
76: 
77: **Where our promises cost us marks, by choice.** Curio Class will not build some things rubrics ask for
78: (FOUNDATION, section 3): data dashboards (Texas names one as an example), adapting to each student and plugging
79: into a learning management system (Ohio, narrative items), accessible-format files lodged with a national centre
80: (Oregon), and professional learning tied to the materials (Ohio, scored). Our answers are the class record sheet,
81: score codes, open pages, print and teacher pages. Professional learning: none for now (decided 2026-11-11). It is
82: a future area of growth, not a closed door.
83: 
84: **Where the instruments conflict on values.** Oklahoma's arts rubric checks materials against state law. Maryland,
85: the federal health tool and the SEL registry ask for equity content in terms that pull the other way. No stance
86: passes all of them. C12 follows FOUNDATION promise 7, a middle line, and Curio Class does not claim to satisfy any
87: state's values criteria. Said once here, so it is not re-opened per course. Cory's values rule (2026-11-12) now
88: settles these conflicts in order: the higher court, then the evidence, then dignity and openness, then the age of
89: the student (see "Still open with Cory", b, and social studies item 11). FOUNDATION's promises still bind.
90: 
91: **Acceptability is local.** A national site cannot settle what suits each community, so each course page says what
92: a school may leave out. For health this is decided: all sensitive topics are carried, and each sensitive unit can
93: be left out without breaking the course.
