194: ## After-core courses (added 2026-11-25)
195: The after-core subjects (arts, health, physical education, computer science, career education, SEL, ELD, library)
196: use this brief with these changes. Their own pages outrank the core lessons above where they differ.
197: 
198: - **Read first, in place of items 2, 6 and 7:** the subject's docs/courses/<subject>/STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md in
199:   full, docs/agents/lenses/<subject>.md, the subject's row in docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md ("Before a scope and
200:   sequence in this subject") and its bar part, every DESK-ANSWERS.md entry that names the subject, and the
201:   subject's stage 1 and progression reviews (docs/reviews/prework-<subject>-*, progression-<subject>-*). The core
202:   frames the course leans on are read at their Units tables only (docs/courses/<core course>/frame.md).
203: - **The rows:** the course's `rows` cell in data/plans/course-list.csv selects them (`file:` the progression file,
204:   `grade:` the grades, `strand:` the strand, `|` between values). `python -X utf8 scripts/frame.py check <slug>`
205:   checks every one is placed once.
206: - **Band courses** (one course for several grade years, or the high school arts levels): one frame file for the
207:   band. The Units table gains a `Year` column right after `#` (`K`, `1`, `2`, or `Level 1` and so on), units are
208:   numbered through the band (unit 1 of year 2 follows the last unit of year 1), and each year is whole on its own:
209:   its own units, its own end-of-unit assessments and its own mid-year and year-end course-level checks. The
210:   **Days.** bullet gives the band's total first, then the days a year ("192: 64 a year for three years").
211:   `frame.py` spreads the band's days over that many school years and notes the unit days by year. A subject page
212:   that says "one frame per grade year" is met by this shape: each year's part of the file is that year's frame, and
213:   the scope and sequence later writes one year at a time.
214: - **Doses:** use the minutes, sessions a week and days a year the subject's STANDARD.md and APPROACH.md settle; never
215:   the core 160-day yardstick unless the subject sets it. A smaller course means fewer minutes a week, never fewer
216:   units (Cory 2026-11-15).
217: - **Course-level checks:** grades K to 8 after-core checks do not take the core subjects' target weeks; place them
218:   so they do not fall in a week (K to 5) where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a core check for that grade, and say
219:   which weeks.
220: - **The seventh Units column** (lead 2026-11-25, from the pilot fix passes): arts heads it "Processes, anchors and
221:   works", physical education "Cues and settings", health "Skill steps", SEL "Core link and routines", computer
222:   science "Practices and concept areas", career education "Practices", ELD "Host unit and key language uses", library
223:   "Inquiry phases".
224: - **Owned standards cell:** a unit that owns no code (a carry-only or removable sensitive unit) says `None`; the
225:   check reads `None` and an empty cell alike.
226: - **A course-level check right after a unit** is that unit's end-of-unit assessment when it covers the unit, as in
227:   the core frames; say so in the unit's row and the check's "What it checks" cell. Units not followed by a check end
228:   in their own end-of-unit assessment.
229: - **Grades 6 to 12:** the year-end check may sit in week 32, the finals week (CALENDAR.md stagger rules 3 and 4).
230: - **After-core stagger:** each after-core course counts on its own in `frame.py calendar` (the five arts courses of a
231:   band are five checks, not one). Writers keep checks off core check weeks; the lead runs one calendar pass across
232:   the after-core frames for clashes among them.
233: - **Gates the subject sets:** a subject page that holds building until a condition is met (ELD: C11 proven in the
234:   core courses) still gets its frame now (Cory 2026-11-25: "please see if you can get them all completed"); the
235:   frame says in **For the lead** which later stage the condition holds.

FILE docs/courses/sel/STANDARD.md
50: 
51: - **The catalog.** data/courses.csv has four SEL band rows (`sel-k-2`, `sel-3-5`, `sel-6-8`, `sel-9-12`; Cory,
52:   2026-11-21, "Light shelf" dropped), as docs/COURSES-STATUS.md counts them. The bar asks for a sequence for each grade, and the registry gives a
53:   single lesson set for a band its lower design rating. So each band course holds one distinct year per grade, and
54:   "a band taught the same way every year" is a fail (bar, Fails if).
55: - **How a grade year is framed.** Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-sel-eld): each grade year is
56:   framed whole, with its own year of units. Since 2026-11-25 (Cory, docs/DESK-ANSWERS.md) a band has one frame with a
57:   Year column, and its scope and sequence is written one year at a time. plan.py takes one course id per band (the
58:   four catalog ids).
59: - **Sessions.** Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-sel-eld): two sessions a week in every grade, 32 weeks,
60:   so 64 sessions a year. A session is 20 minutes in K to 2, 30 in grades 3 to 5, 30 in grades 6 to 8 and 40 in
61:   grades 9 to 12 (an advisory period); a frame may change its band's number and gives the reason. The registry's
62:   criteria ask that sessions a year be stated and set no number (criteria paper p 25, section 4), so the course page
63:   states ours with no national figure to compare. The Education Endowment Foundation's toolkit entry for social and
64:   emotional learning finds shorter sessions, up to 45 minutes, twice a week, the most successful structure for
65:   interventions (web page, read 2026-11-21); our minutes sit inside that. "Two 30-minute sessions a week" is not on
66:   that entry or on the primary guidance report's web page (read 2026-11-21) and is not used; the report itself is a
67:   file to download, unread. One session a week (32 sessions) is the stated short path (C2), and
68:   it never drops a unit.
69: - **How the sessions add up** (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-sel-1). Related sub-skills are taught together, so a
70:   unit splits its sub-skills (up to eight) into two or three groups, one section each. Each group is introduced
71:   (Understanding) and then rehearsed (Fluency, Application), and every session rehearses. Full year, 64 = four units
72:   of 11 and two of 10: a three-group unit is 3 groups of 3 sessions (Understanding, Fluency, Application) plus a
73:   Check and a Respond, 11; a two-group unit is 2 groups of 4 (Understanding, two Fluency, Application) plus a Check
74:   and a Respond, 10. The frame says which four units take three groups (most often those with the most sub-skills)
75:   and may move a session between units if the year stays 64. Short path, 32 = four units of 5 and two of 6, all six
76:   units and every group kept, fewer minutes a unit, never fewer units: each group's Understanding and Fluency share
77:   one session, the unit's Application sessions become one, and the Check and Respond share one. A three-group unit
78:   is 3 + 1 + 1 = 5; a two-group unit keeps a second rehearsal per group, 2 x 2 + 1 + 1 = 6. So the short path cuts
79:   Fluency and Application sessions and joins the Check with the Respond; it drops no sub-skill, group or unit.
80: - **Units.** Six units per grade year (section 3), each with its own readiness check and end-of-unit assessment,
81:   sized to the minutes chosen. "Never fewer units" is met: every sub-skill is taught, practiced spaced out and
82:   checked every year.
83: - **Spanish.** Family notes in English and Spanish per unit (decided 2026-11-11); student pages stay English only
84:   for now (settled 2026-11-16, Cory, Desk card q-later-sel-eld). Growing Spanish across the site is a later roadmap
85:   item (the lead's note).
86: 
87: ## 3. The sequence per grade
88: 
89: The draft year for each grade. The theme sets the year's angle, so no two years of a band run alike. Each unit lists
90: the sub-skills it teaches first that year; every later unit brings earlier sub-skills back in practice (C7). In
91: every grade all 40 sub-skills are taught once and only once as a unit's own. No unit teaches more than eight
92: sub-skills first, so each has time to be rehearsed (a unit has 10 or 11 sessions, or the sizes its band frame sets to fit course-level checks, its sub-skills taught in two or
93: three groups of related skills, each group introduced then rehearsed: section 2, "How the sessions add up"); a sub-skill that suits a late
94: unit better comes back there in practice, not as a first teaching.
95: 
96: **Depth ladder, by band.** K to 2: a picture or story moment, one or two pictured steps, said aloud or acted with a
97: teacher; the skill is named. Grades 3 to 5: a named strategy with three or four steps and a reason, used on an
98: invented case and then in a planned class moment. Grades 6 to 8: the strategy chosen among options and weighed
99: against real pressures (friends, online, time), with the reason written down. Grades 9 to 12: a plan the student
100: designs for an invented or chosen situation, with its trade-offs and a judgement of how well it worked. Within a
101: band the first year uses cases from the school day, the second adds a second person's point of view, and the third
102: adds a cost or a pressure; grade 12, the fourth year of 9 to 12, adds a setting past school (work, community, life
103: after school). The progression row for each sub-skill says which step a year is.
104: 
105: **Ownership, for the progression** (settled 2026-11-21: Cory asked for a pedagogical check of the recommendation and
106: its use if it held; it held with two fixes, docs/reviews/sel-ownership-check-2026-11-21.md). One owned row per
107: sub-skill per band, so 40 sub-skills by four bands is 160 owned rows. A band's owner year is its last grade (2, 5, 8
108: and 12), the year the sub-skill is met at the band's full depth. Every grade year still has its own row for all 40
109: sub-skills (520 rows in all), and the other years of the band carry the id as `~ID`. A `~` row is a full teaching
110: row, not a lesser one: it has its own words, steps and exit ticket at its year's depth, and a one-line note on how
111: that year differs. Each row's `assumes` names the same sub-skill in the year before (a `~` row or an owner row;
112: kindergarten assumes nothing for it), so a band's later years build on its earlier ones and the first year of a band
113: builds on the previous band's owner row, with a note on the step up. The grade table below stays the placement, and
114: every year still teaches all 40 once. Other subjects' leans name the SEL row of the grade whose depth they need.
115: scripts/progression.py checks this form (MULTI_OWNER: one owner per id in each of grades 2, 5, 8 and 12); it has
116: supported SEL since 2026-11-21, and data/progressions/sel.csv passes it.
117: 
118: How each competency is practiced (bar item 5): self-management and relationship skills by doing (rehearsing a
119: calming routine, acting out an invented hard moment, practising an I-statement with a partner); self-awareness,
120: social awareness and decision-making by reflection and talk (a private journal, partner talk about a scenario, a
121: choice weighed in writing).
