138: 
139: Unit rules:
140: 
141: - **Kindergarten unit 1 assumes nothing.** Its readiness item checks only what a five-year-old arrives with
142:   (naming a classmate, taking a turn), and no later unit is assumed by it.
143: - **Cases stay in school, friend, family-chore, sport and online settings.** No invented case names self-harm, a
144:   diagnosis, dating, sex or a substance. The units that sit next to health, every grade 6 to 12 unit that teaches
145:   SM2 (coping with stress) or RS6 (saying no to pressure) first (among them "Stress and strong feelings", "Under
146:   pressure", "Stress and coping", "Saying no, setting limits", "Help, support and pressure" and "Steady under
147:   change"), name the health unit in the teacher notes and hand those topics to it.
148: - **Grade 10 unit 4, "Saying no, setting limits"** (retitled from "Healthy boundaries", Cory, 2026-11-21), so the
149:   title does not read as dating or sexual health.
150: 
151: Placement notes, by sub-skill, where the grade matters most:
152: 
153: 
154: - **SA6 (own snap judgements) and SO6 (norms that have been unfair)** start in K to 2 as fairness
155:   at play ("everyone can join"), and reach unfair rules of the past in grade 5 and later, taught from settled facts
156:   (see section 5). In grades 3 and 4 SO6 is a rule in a class, club or game that leaves someone out, on invented
157:   cases; SO8 is how a school's or a town's rules and signs shape what people do. Grade 7 ("Snap judgements") and grade 10 ("Culture and respect") are their main years.
158: - **SA8 (ability grows)** is taught as "practice and help make you better", never as a promise that belief alone
159:   raises grades: the evidence for short mindset lessons is mixed (lens, sources; from memory).
160: - **SO8 (systems shape behaviour)** starts as "why our class has rules" and grows to media and online norms in grade
161:   6 and to communities and systems in grade 12.
162: - **RS6 (saying no to pressure)** is taught as a skill on invented cases. The topics where pressure matters most in
163:   6 to 12 (substances, sex, dating violence) are health's; SEL rehearses the refusal skill and points to the health
164:   unit by name in the teacher notes.
165: - **Role play of refusal and standing up (RS6, RS9).** The teacher or a card plays the pressure or the unkind act; no
166:   student plays a person who pressures or is unkind.
167: - **SA1 and SA2 (groups you belong to, strengths from your culture)** use an invented character first. A child may share
168:   or make one up; a family or culture is never asked about by name in a check.
169: - **RS9 (speaking up for others)** stays inside school life: an upstander in bullying or exclusion, a student who
170:   gets help for a friend. It is not a cause the course picks (section 5).
171: - **Transition years** carry the theme: grade 5 "Ready for change", grade 6 "New school, new me", grade 9 "Finding
172:   my footing", grade 12 "Leading and leaving".
173: 
