189: | 1 | 3 | Think, feel, do. *What can I do between a big feeling and what I do next?* | 11 | g3-rds-careful-thinking; g3-rds-good-for-us; g3-sa-feel-think-link; g3-sa-name-feelings; g3-sm-big-feelings; g3-sm-calm-down |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e3-character-actions, how story characters' feelings and motivations drive their actions | Three groups. Group 1, spot it and name it: the feelings faces chart gains a 1 to 5 strength scale and the routine "spot the body clue, name it, rate it", used on school-day scene cards (a lost recess game, a hard spelling quiz), with the reason that naming a feeling first makes it easier to handle (SA3); a think-feel-do chain for a character whose classmate did not wave back, then "catch the thought, test it, try another" and a second thought that fits the facts (SA5). Group 2, calm it, taught calm-down first: the calm-down steps grow to four, stop, breathe, name it, choose, on a plan card with three body tools (slow breaths, a stretch, counting back from ten) for two school moments, rehearsed daily for a week (the carry-out cue) with a reason for each step (SM2); the four steps before acting when a teammate grabs the ball, and the difference between reacting and responding in a sentence (SM1). Group 3, choose with care: stop-think-choose grows to four steps, stop, list two or three choices, think what each would cause, choose and check; two invented classmates doing the same task, one who rushed and one who used the steps, and one place at home the steps would help (RD5); two options for a recess choice (finish a game indoors, or play with a classmate who is alone) weighed for one's own well-being and others', and a pick with a reason (RD6). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 14 and 18: the calm-down tool choice, hidden feelings, careful thinking) in the openings; unit 1 opens the band, so where the class did not have SEL K to 2 the first Understanding session teaches the grade 2 card forms first (Leave-out). End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (a character's feeling, its clue and strength; a second thought that fits the facts; the step a character skipped) and the four calm-down steps rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: "stop, breathe, name it, choose" before transitions. Family activity: practice the plan card's three body tools at home when calm. Tools: the feelings faces chart with its scale, the calm-down plan card, the four-step stop-think-choose card, the think-feel-do chain sheet, scene cards. Curio Class writes the chart scale, both cards, the chain sheet, the scene cards, the page and the family note. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Out: a student's own feelings in a check (never asked); two characters' chains (unit 10). Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Self-Awareness.3, CASEL.Self-Awareness.5, CASEL.Self-Management.1, CASEL.Self-Management.2, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6. | First because it grows three cards the year uses: the strength scale (unit 3), the calm-down steps (every opening) and stop-think-choose (units 2, 3 and 5). It runs beside ELA grade 3 unit 1, where characters' feelings and actions are read. Six sub-skills, three of them new card forms, so three groups and 11 sessions (one of the year's four 11s). Health grade 3 unit 4 (weeks 17 to 21) uses these feelings words later (Cross-subject). Sessions 1 to 11 of the year, weeks 1 to 6. | Candidate: the plan card's three body tools checked against health 3 to 5's calm tools so one card serves both; the "test it" step's look-for written so it never asks a student to test their own thought aloud | No clinical words; "catch the thought, test it" is a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment, and no student tests their own thoughts aloud. Feelings are named for characters in checks. Body tools are plain body skills with no spiritual framing; mental health topics are health's. Stretches standing beside desks with room to reach. Disclosure: health's steps in every unit (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something": the school's reporting route and the counselor). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (body tools). |
190: | 2 | 3 | Who I am and what I bring. *What makes up who a person is, and what does each person bring to a class?* | 8 | g3-sa-my-strengths; g3-sa-tell-truth; g3-sa-what-interests-me; g3-sa-who-i-am; g3-soc-notice-good |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:ss3-biography, the achievements and contributions people make | Two groups. The private interest log starts on session 1 and runs two weeks (the carry-out cue): what the student chose to do in free time, and why. Group 1, who I am: an identity web for an invented character (a team, a helper role at home, a club, a language) in three steps, list the groups, say what each adds, say where each part shows, and why knowing it helps; a private web of their own if they choose (SA1); a strengths hunt in three steps, recall a task that went well, name the skill it used, find another place it helps, for a story character first, a language spoken counting as a strength, each backed by an example (SA2). Group 2, what I stand for and bring: the honest action on a school-day case (a classmate's answer sheet left in view) chosen with stop-think-choose, the value named, and what honesty cost and kept (SA4); one entry picked from the private log and a purpose named for it (SA9); a three-part shout-out after a planned partner task, what they did, how, why it mattered, and a vague "good job" rewritten (SO2). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 13, 14 and 17) and unit 1's stop-think-choose in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year course-level check after this unit is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks): an unseen page (the honest action and its value; a strength with its example; a shout-out rewritten) and a shout-out rehearsed with a partner from an unseen task card. Carry it out: three-part shout-outs at the end of group work. Family activity: build an identity web for a made-up character together, or a real one if the family chooses. Tools: the web sheet, the strengths hunt card, the private log, stop-think-choose, task cards. Curio Class writes the model webs, the case, the log page and the family note. Provisional cost for 30: about $9 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Self-Awareness.1, CASEL.Self-Awareness.2, CASEL.Self-Awareness.4, CASEL.Self-Awareness.9, CASEL.Social Awareness.2. | After unit 1 because the honest choice uses the four-step stop-think-choose; before unit 3 because a student names their own groups and strengths before reading others'. Five sub-skills, mostly reflection and talk, so two groups and 10 sessions, its Check and Respond being the mid-year check. Social studies grade 3 unit 7 (biographies, course days 141 to 160) comes later in the year (Cross-subject). Sessions 12 to 19 of the year, weeks 6 to 10; the mid-year check is sessions 20 and 21. | Candidate: the two-week log's "steps followed" look-for written on the class sheet; the model webs checked for range (families of every kind, a home language, a helper role) | Invented character first; a student may make up their own web; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check. Home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about. SA4 is checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct. The log and private web stay with the student. Agent reads: sensitivity (the agent first studies an education association's published guidance on families and cultures in the classroom). |
191: | 3 | 3 | Walking in their shoes. *How can I tell what someone else feels and sees, and what is the kind thing to do?* | 11 | g3-rel-different-ways; g3-sa-snap-judgments; g3-soc-care-when-struggle; g3-soc-read-feelings; g3-soc-thankful; g3-soc-their-view; g3-soc-why-rules |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e3-point-of-view, telling one's own view apart from a character's | Three groups. The class gratitude wall starts on session 1 and runs two weeks (the carry-out cue). Group 1, reading others: three kinds of clue (face, body, voice) read in short teacher-acted school scenes, the strength rated on the feelings faces scale, the guess checked with a question (SO4); the "shoes switch" on a school-day scene card, what each person saw, wants and why each acted, then the scene retold from the other side (SO1); three care steps, notice, ask what would help, do it, acted with a partner for a character who lost a library book, and why asking first beats guessing (SO3). Group 2, checking my guess: a three-step check on a snap judgment (a quiet new student called stuck-up), catch the first thought, ask what I actually know, find out before deciding, with stop-think-choose, and why first guesses mislead (SA6); with an invented story partner from another place on a shared class task, three steps, notice a different way, ask about it with respect, try it or combine it; the partner's way is a way of keeping score or taking turns in a game from home, the place and the way taken by the unit plan from a cited source (RS3). Group 3, thanks and systems: each gratitude note names the act, the person and the difference it made, and why noticing good things changes a class (SO5); one school system (the lunch line) followed in three steps, the rule, what it makes people do, what would happen without it (SO8). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 13, 15, 16 and 18) and unit 1's strength scale and stop-think-choose in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (three clues and a checking question; the other side's reason; the snap-judgment check) and the care steps rehearsed from an unseen card, the teacher playing the one who struggles. Carry it out: "ask, don't guess" in group work. Family activity: retell a family story from another person's side, real or made up. Tools: the feelings faces chart, the shoes switch card, the care steps card, the gratitude wall, scene cards, the story partner page. Curio Class writes the scenes, the story partner page from its source, the cards and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.3, CASEL.Self-Awareness.6, CASEL.Social Awareness.1, CASEL.Social Awareness.3, CASEL.Social Awareness.4, CASEL.Social Awareness.5, CASEL.Social Awareness.8. | After unit 1 because reading others uses its strength scale and the snap-judgment check uses its stop-think-choose; after unit 2 because a student reads others after naming their own groups. Seven sub-skills, so three groups and 11 sessions. ELA grade 3 unit 3 (point of view, course days 81 to 119) comes after; social studies grade 3 unit 2 (rules and rights, course days 25 to 48) comes before (Cross-subject). Sessions 22 to 32 of the year, weeks 11 to 16. | Candidate: the teacher-acted scenes scripted in the unit plan; the story partner's game way taken from a source the sensitivity agent has read, not one of PE's or arts' grades 3 to 5 picks | SA6 on invented cases, the wrong guess resting on a neutral trait (quiet, new), never on skin, hair, dress, faith or accent; never blame of a group. RS3: no student is asked to explain their own culture; the partner's way is one invented child's, from a cited source. If a student tells of harm or risk in a care scene, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity (the agent first studies the source for the partner's way). |
192: | 4 | 3 | When friends disagree. *What helps friends talk it out, fix a hurt and choose together?* | 10 | g3-rel-ask-offer-help; g3-rel-friendships; g3-rel-lead-follow; g3-rel-settle-disagreements; g3-rel-talk-listen; g3-soc-read-situation |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e3-discussion, the same class talk norms and words | Two groups. Group 1, talk it out and repair, taught in this order because each uses the one before: the talk turn card grows to four steps, listen, say back what you heard, ask a question, add your idea, used in a planned partner talk on a friend-disagreement case, with the reason for saying back first (RS1); the I-statement grows to four parts, "I feel ___ when ___ because ___. I need ___." (STANDARD section 7), and "cool, talk, choose" on an invented recess disagreement, both partners saying an I-statement, then a fair solution picked from at least two (RS5); friendship actions sorted into "builds trust" and "breaks trust", and a broken-trust moment repaired in three steps, own it, say sorry with the I-statement, make a plan, rehearsed with a partner playing the friend who was hurt (RS2). Group 2, help, places and leading: the problem size meter gains a fourth level (tiny, small, medium, big), school-day problems sized from a card deck, asking a trusted adult about a big one rehearsed in three steps (go to them, say what happened, say what you need) with a partner playing the adult, and help offered on a small one (RS8); a school situation read with three questions, where am I, what does this place expect, what chance does it give me, on the start of a group project and a fire drill, and the fitting response acted (SO7); leading a small-group disagreement case with three steps, hear every idea with the talk turn card, sum up, help the group choose, then following another leader's choice (RS7). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 13 and 15) and unit 1's calm-down steps in the openings; health grade 3 unit 3's help-steps ("pick the right helper") open RS8 as retrieval where the school runs health, and where it does not this unit teaches the asking from the meter. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (a problem sized and its helper; a fair solution from two; a situation's three questions answered) and a four-part I-statement rehearsed from an unseen card, the teacher playing the other part. Carry it out: "say back first" in every class talk. Family activity: use the four-part I-statement once at home on a small thing. Tools: the four-step talk turn card, the four-part I-statement frame, the four-level problem size meter, the trust sort cards, scene cards. Curio Class writes the three cards, the problem deck, the cases and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.1, CASEL.Relationship Skills.2, CASEL.Relationship Skills.5, CASEL.Relationship Skills.7, CASEL.Relationship Skills.8, CASEL.Social Awareness.7. | After unit 3 because talking out a disagreement uses reading the other side; it grows the talk turn card, I-statement and meter that units 5 and 6 use. Six sub-skills, mostly rehearsed by doing, in two groups of three with two Fluency sessions each, so 10 sessions. ELA grade 3 unit 1 set the talk norms earlier; health grade 3 unit 3 (weeks 10 to 14) taught which helper fits which problem; health grade 3 unit 4 (weeks 17 to 21) teaches its I-statement in the same weeks (Cross-subject; For the lead 4). Sessions 33 to 42 of the year, weeks 17 to 21. | Candidate: the I-statement and listening wording settled with health grade 3 unit 4 before either unit plan is written (For the lead 4) | No real friends are named. The teacher plays any upset or hurt part. RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps, named because asking for help can bring one. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
193: | 5 | 3 | Saying no, choosing well. *How do I hold my ground and choose well when others push or a choice is hard?* | 9 | g3-rds-ask-questions; g3-rds-check-facts; g3-rds-solve-problems; g3-rds-think-ahead; g3-rds-who-is-affected; g3-rel-say-no; g3-rel-stand-up; g3-soc-fair-rules |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): math:m3-scaled-graphs, reading a scaled bar graph of class data | Three groups. Group 1, think ahead and hold my ground: what each of two choices on a school-day card causes next, later that day and next week, with stop-think-choose, and the result judged after (RD4); a four-step refusal, stop and think, say no clearly, give a reason, offer something else or walk away, rehearsed when a card character urges copying homework or sneaking into the closed gym, and why a reason makes a no stronger (RS6); an upstander plan with four options, speak up with an I-statement, stand with the one left out, get help, check in after, the fitting option picked for each of two school cases of teasing or exclusion with a reason, and the chosen words rehearsed with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act (RS9). Group 2, questions, facts and fair rules: three kinds of question (what, why, what if) asked of a scene-card case before a class choice, and a first idea changed when a new fact comes in (RD1); a scaled bar graph of a class survey (which game for indoor recess) made and read, the choice made from it, saying which fact decided and which opinion did not (RD2); an invented club rule ("only kids who own a ball may play") checked with three fairness questions, who can join, who is left out, how could it change, and rewritten (SO6). Group 3, solve it and see who it touches: a classroom problem (crowded coat hooks) solved in four steps, size it on the meter, list ideas, choose one, test it for a week (the carry-out cue) and check (RD3); who a class choice affects placed on a three-ring "who does it touch?" circle (me, my class, the school) with a reason each, and one school rule it must follow (RD7). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 13, 16 and 18) and unit 4's I-statement and meter in the openings; health grade 3 unit 4's upstander line opens RS9 as retrieval where the school runs health. End-of-unit evidence: the year-end course-level check after this unit is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks): an unseen scene page (results along time; a fact told from an opinion on a scaled graph; a rule rewritten) and the four-step refusal rehearsed with the card playing the push. Carry it out: "what, why, what if" before class votes. Family activity: make a scaled tally of a family vote and choose from it. Tools: stop-think-choose, the refusal card, the upstander plan card, the I-statement frame, the problem size meter, a graph sheet, the "who does it touch?" circle. Curio Class writes the cards, cases, survey sheet and page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.6, CASEL.Relationship Skills.9, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7, CASEL.Social Awareness.6. | After unit 4 because speaking up uses its I-statement and the coat-hook problem its meter. Eight sub-skills, so three groups and 11 sessions, its Check and Respond being the year-end check. The scaled graph leans on math grade 3 unit 1 (weeks 1 to 5); health grade 3 unit 4 (weeks 17 to 21) taught what bullying is first (Cross-subject). Sessions 43 to 51 of the year, weeks 22 to 26; the year-end check is sessions 52 and 53. | Candidate: the coat-hook test timed so its check falls before session 51; the refusal cases kept to school-day rule breaks, checked against health 3 to 5's refusal steps (For the lead 4) | The teacher or a card plays the pressure and the unkind act; no student plays the one who pushes, is unkind or is left out. School life only, no cause chosen by the course. Substance and body-safety topics are health's. SO6: rules in a class, club or game on invented cases; unfair rules of the past wait for grade 5; no current dispute. Teasing can bring a disclosure: health's steps and the school's bullying route. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
194: | 6 | 3 | Goals that stretch me. *How do I reach a goal that is hard for me, and how does our class reach one together?* | 11 | g3-rel-teamwork; g3-sa-i-can-try; g3-sa-skills-grow; g3-sm-have-a-say; g3-sm-plan-and-tidy; g3-sm-set-goal; g3-sm-step-forward; g3-sm-stay-on-task |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:ss3-taking-part, how people take part in a democracy (SEL: the student's own voice in class) | Three groups. The three-week goal and the three-week practice record start on session 1 (the carry-out cues) and end before the Check session. Group 1, plan the stretch: the goal steps card grows to four steps, goal, steps, block and plan, check, and a three-week school goal (finish a chapter book) checked weekly with one step changed (SM4); a stretch goal planned for an invented character on the card, with a likely block, a helper and an "if this happens, then I will" plan (SA7); a three-week practice record of one school skill (multiplication facts or a jump-rope count, practiced in the class's own time or in PE), which practice or help made the biggest change, growth explained as practice, strategy and help, never belief alone (SA8). Group 2, our class plan: the class proposes and votes on one change to a class routine, each student giving a reason with evidence, and carries it out for two weeks (SM7); a three-step focus plan, spot the distraction, say the cue, move seats or ask, with a try-again step for when the work gets hard (try another way, then ask), checked off on the goal steps card in a planned work time (SM3); a one-week class project planned on a planner with days and times and a materials checklist, reviewed at the end (SM5). Group 3, step up together: a class need noticed (a welcome kit for new students), the first step planned on the goal steps card and started without being asked (SM6); groups rotate four roles (leader, recorder, checker, timekeeper) on a stretch challenge, a paper tower that holds a book, using the talk turn card, solving one team problem and saying what the group did well (RS4). Retrieval: the grade 2 owner rows (SEL K to 2 units 14, 17 and 18) and unit 4's talk turn card in the openings; health grade 3 unit 5's goal-plan opens SM4 as retrieval where the school runs health. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen page (an if-then plan for a character's block; which practice helped most on an invented record) and a team problem solved with the talk turn card from an unseen card, watched against the look-fors; sampled again by grade 4's mid-year check. The course never records a student's own answer to SA7. Carry it out: the weekly goal check. Family activity: a three-week family practice record of one small skill. Tools: the four-step goal steps card, the practice record, the planner page, role cards, paper and tape. Curio Class writes the card, the record, the planner and the role cards. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing and about $3 for paper and tape. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.4, CASEL.Self-Awareness.7, CASEL.Self-Awareness.8, CASEL.Self-Management.3, CASEL.Self-Management.4, CASEL.Self-Management.5, CASEL.Self-Management.6, CASEL.Self-Management.7. | Last because it gathers the year's cards into goals and a team challenge and hands every grade 3 row on to grade 4. Eight sub-skills, so three groups and 11 sessions; its six weeks hold the three-week trackers. Health grade 3 unit 5 (weeks 22 to 24) sets a health goal first; social studies grade 3 unit 2 (course days 25 to 48) taught taking part (Cross-subject). Sessions 54 to 64 of the year, weeks 27 to 32. | Candidate: the routine-change vote run as group 2's Application so its two weeks end before session 63; the welcome kit made from paper the class has | Growth is practice and help, never belief alone. SA7 plans are a character's; a student's own plan is private. No family is asked to buy supplies. The paper tower stays on desks with books stacked low; no standing on chairs. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (paper tower). |
195: | 7 | 4 | Team rules we make. *How does a team agree on rules that are fair to every member?* | 10 | g4-rel-talk-listen; g4-rel-teamwork; g4-sa-tell-truth; g4-soc-fair-rules; g4-soc-read-situation |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e4-discussion, the same class talk norms and roles | Two groups. Group 1, our working agreement: before the class agrees a team norm, the student says back a partner's view and then their own with the talk turn card, and checks the partner feels heard (RS1); teams write a working agreement (how we decide, how we share jobs) and use it on a group build, naming how a teammate saw a problem differently and how the team used both ideas (RS4); what a team situation asks of each member (a group presentation, a substitute teacher day), compared for two roles (SO7). Group 2, fair and honest teams: invented club rules reviewed from a newcomer's and a longtime member's side with the three fairness questions, and a version both could accept agreed (SO6); a teammate who wants to hide a mistake from the teacher, the honest action picked with stop-think-choose, how the teammate sees it, and a way to be honest that respects them (SA4). Retrieval: the grade 3 rows (units 2, 4, 5 and 6) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (a club rule from two sides; the honest action that respects a teammate) and say-back-then-view rehearsed in a planned partner talk against the look-fors. Carry it out: "say back, then your view" before any class vote. Family activity: write a family working agreement for one shared job. Tools: the talk turn card, stop-think-choose, the agreement sheet, build supplies, role cards. Curio Class writes the agreement sheet, the club rules and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $9 printing and about $5 for paper, straws and tape. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.1, CASEL.Relationship Skills.4, CASEL.Self-Awareness.4, CASEL.Social Awareness.6, CASEL.Social Awareness.7. | First because the year's angle is working together, and every later team task in grade 4 runs on the agreement made here; it runs beside ELA grade 4 unit 1's discussion roles. Five sub-skills, two groups and 10 sessions. PE grades 3 to 5 unit 11 (weeks 9 to 13) teaches team roles after it (Cross-subject). Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: the group build kept to one session of building so the agreement, not the build, is the point | SA4 is checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct. SO6: invented class, club or game rules only; no current dispute. Child-safe scissors only; build on desks. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (group build). |
196: | 8 | 4 | Plans and steps. *How does a team turn a goal into a plan that works for everyone in it?* | 10 | g4-rds-careful-thinking; g4-sa-i-can-try; g4-sm-plan-and-tidy; g4-sm-set-goal; g4-sm-stay-on-task; g4-sm-step-forward |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e4-writing-process, planning, drafting and revising with feedback | Two groups. A two-week team project (a class guide for new students) starts on session 1 and carries both groups. Group 1, a shared plan: a shared goal set with a partner on the goal steps card, each with their own steps, and a step changed after hearing what blocks the partner (SM4); the project split into a timeline with deadlines and who does what, each student keeping a materials list and comparing at review how a teammate planned the same job (SM5); a first step proposed for the project, a teammate's view asked before starting, and how it changed the start (SM6). Group 2, two paths and focus: two invented characters with the same hard goal, two paths planned on the goal steps card each with a helper, and why each fits (SA7); the focus plan and its try-again step used across the project, checked in with a partner, what pulls each off task compared and one tip swapped (SM3); the team plan reviewed with stop-think-choose, a teammate saying where careful thinking caught a mistake, and one place outside school it would help (RD5). Retrieval: the grade 3 rows (units 1 and 6) in the openings; health grade 4 unit 8's goal plan opens SM4 as retrieval where the school runs health. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen page (two paths for two characters; a step changed for a partner's block) and the focus check-in rehearsed with a partner from an unseen card. The course never records a student's own answer to SA7. Carry it out: a partner focus check halfway through work time. Family activity: plan one family job as a timeline with who does what. Tools: the goal steps card, the timeline sheet, the focus plan card, stop-think-choose. Curio Class writes the sheets, the two-character case and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5, CASEL.Self-Awareness.7, CASEL.Self-Management.3, CASEL.Self-Management.4, CASEL.Self-Management.5, CASEL.Self-Management.6. | After unit 7 because a team plan runs on the working agreement. Six sub-skills in two groups of three, each with two Fluency sessions, so 10 sessions; the two-week project fits its five weeks. Health grade 4 unit 8 (weeks 1 to 4) teaches its goal plan first (Cross-subject). Sessions 11 to 20 of the year, weeks 6 to 10. | Candidate: the class guide project sized so its two weeks end before the Check session; the try-again step worded the same as unit 6's (AASL.V.D.1, Library) | Plans for invented characters; a student's own plan is private. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
197: | 9 | 4 | Many ways to be. *How can two people see the same thing so differently, and what can each learn from the other?* | 8 | g4-rel-different-ways; g4-sa-my-strengths; g4-sa-snap-judgments; g4-sa-who-i-am; g4-soc-notice-good; g4-soc-their-view |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): social-studies:ss4-regional-cultures, regions with different ways of life | Two groups. Group 1, webs and strengths: two invented characters' identity webs compared, what they share and what differs, and the student may add to their own private web (SA1); a strength a story character brings from her culture or language, and a strength a teammate saw in the student, the two views of a strength compared (SA2); specific feedback that names a strength a classmate may not see in themselves, and how they see it asked (SO2). Group 2, two views: a school scene retold from two characters' points of view with the shoes switch, and what each misunderstood about the other written (SO1); two characters who judge a third differently, what each assumed found, and what to find out decided with stop-think-choose (SA6); with an invented story partner whose family cooks or shares work differently (who does which part of a shared meal), a plan to blend two ways of doing a class task, and how the partner sees the blend asked; the place and the way taken by the unit plan from a cited source (RS3). Retrieval: the grade 3 rows (units 2 and 3) in the openings. End-of-unit evidence: the mid-year course-level check after this unit is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks): an unseen page (two webs compared; what two characters misunderstood; two snap judgments and what to find out) and an unseen-strength feedback rehearsed with a partner. Carry it out: "what might they see that I don't?" before a group decides. Family activity: compare two family members' ways of doing one chore, made up if the family chooses. Tools: two model webs, the shoes switch card, stop-think-choose, the story partner page. Curio Class writes the webs, the scene, the partner page from its source and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $9 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.3, CASEL.Self-Awareness.1, CASEL.Self-Awareness.2, CASEL.Self-Awareness.6, CASEL.Social Awareness.1, CASEL.Social Awareness.2. | After unit 8 because the year's second-person angle grows from shared plans to shared views. Six sub-skills, two groups and 10 sessions, its Check and Respond being the mid-year check, which must fall in week 15 (Days). Social studies grade 4 unit 7 (regional cultures, course days 137 to 160) comes later; ELA grade 4 unit 1 (narration and point of view) came before (Cross-subject). Sessions 21 to 28 of the year, weeks 11 to 14; the mid-year check is sessions 29 and 30. | Candidate: the story partner's shared-work custom taken from a source the sensitivity agent has read, a different place from units 3 and 17 | Invented characters first; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check; a student may make up their own web. No student is asked to explain their own culture. SA6 on invented cases, a neutral trait, never blame of a group. Agent reads: sensitivity (the agent first studies the guidance on families and cultures named in unit 2 and the partner's source). |
198: | 10 | 4 | Feelings at work. *What happens when two people feel different things about the same moment, and how can I help?* | 11 | g4-sa-feel-think-link; g4-sa-name-feelings; g4-sm-big-feelings; g4-sm-calm-down; g4-soc-care-when-struggle; g4-soc-read-feelings; g4-soc-thankful; g4-soc-why-rules |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e4-character-depth, a character described from thoughts, words and actions | Three groups. Group 1, one event, two people: feelings named and rated for two characters in one team scene on the feelings faces scale, and why the same event made them feel differently (SA3); two think-feel-do chains for two characters after a team loss, different thoughts leading to different feelings and actions (SA5); a team scene where two strong feelings collide, the calm-down steps used, and how the response lands on the teammate (SM1). Group 2, calm and care for a teammate: a calm-down plan for a team moment (a project falling behind), a partner coached through theirs with the cue word, noticing which tool works for whom (SM2); the care steps for a struggling teammate, adapted when the teammate wants something different (SO3); two people's clues read, and a check-in that fits the person (quietly, later, with a question) (SO4). Group 3, thanks and systems: thanks written from a helper's side, what helping cost them and what it made possible (SO5); one school or town system (recess rules, a bike lane) traced for how it shapes what two people do, a student and a crossing guard, and why each might see it differently (SO8). Retrieval: the grade 3 rows (units 1 and 3) in the openings; health grade 4 unit 10's calm tool pick opens SM2 as retrieval where the school runs health. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (two feelings and two chains for one event; a check-in that fits) and the calm-down plan coached with a partner from an unseen card. Carry it out: "how did it land?" after a team disagreement. Family activity: two family members say how one event felt to each, real or made up. Tools: the feelings faces chart, the calm-down plan card, the chain sheet, the care steps card, scene cards. Curio Class writes the scenes, cases and page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Self-Awareness.3, CASEL.Self-Awareness.5, CASEL.Self-Management.1, CASEL.Self-Management.2, CASEL.Social Awareness.3, CASEL.Social Awareness.4, CASEL.Social Awareness.5, CASEL.Social Awareness.8. | After unit 9 because two people's feelings follow two people's views. Eight sub-skills, three groups and 11 sessions. Health grade 4 unit 10 (weeks 10 to 13) uses the calm-down card first, and unit 11 (weeks 16 to 19) shows one thing affecting people differently in the same weeks (Cross-subject). Sessions 31 to 41 of the year, weeks 16 to 21. | Candidate: the crossing-guard case written so a rural and a city school can both use it | No clinical words; mental health topics are health's. Feelings are a character's in checks. Body tools with no spiritual framing. If a student tells of harm or risk in a care scene, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
199: | 11 | 4 | Leading and following. *How can a leader and a follower each help a team, even when the team disagrees?* | 9 | g4-rel-ask-offer-help; g4-rel-friendships; g4-rel-lead-follow; g4-rel-say-no; g4-rel-settle-disagreements; g4-rel-stand-up; g4-sm-have-a-say |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e4-discussion, the facilitator role in a discussion | Three groups. Group 1, leading the team: a team task led with the three leading steps, then followed, and how leader and follower saw the same decision (RS7); the class elects a team to carry out a class improvement, each student weighing a classmate's different proposal before voting (SM7); an invented teammate who needs help, help offered without taking over, and a team problem sized on the meter to know when to bring in a trusted adult, both rehearsed with a partner (RS8). Group 2, repair and settle: two friends who land in different groups, the friend's side heard first, then own it, sorry with the I-statement and a plan, rehearsed with a partner (RS2); a team disagreement where both sides have good reasons, each saying the other's view first, then I-statements and a solution that meets both (RS5). Group 3, hold the no and speak up: the refusal steps when a card character urges the whole group to skip the hard part or blame another team, the no held while the friendship is kept (RS6); how a leader and how a follower could each speak up when a teammate is left out, chosen from the upstander options for each role and rehearsed with the I-statement while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act (RS9). Retrieval: the grade 3 rows (units 4 and 5) in the openings; health grade 4 unit 10's help-steps open RS8 as retrieval where the school runs health. End-of-unit evidence: the year-end course-level check after this unit is this unit's end-of-unit assessment (Course-level checks): an unseen scene page (the other side's view said first; a leader's and a follower's speak-up) and the refusal steps rehearsed with the card playing the group's push. Carry it out: "both views first" when a team splits. Family activity: practice a no that keeps the friendship on a pretend case, the adult playing the pusher. Tools: the talk turn card, the I-statement frame, the problem size meter, the refusal card, the upstander plan card. Curio Class writes the team cases and page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.2, CASEL.Relationship Skills.5, CASEL.Relationship Skills.6, CASEL.Relationship Skills.7, CASEL.Relationship Skills.8, CASEL.Relationship Skills.9, CASEL.Self-Management.7. | After unit 10 because repair and speaking up start from reading how feelings land. Seven sub-skills, mostly rehearsed by doing, so three groups and 11 sessions, its Check and Respond being the year-end check. Health grade 4 unit 12 (weeks 20 to 24) teaches refusal and speak-up on health topics in the same weeks (Cross-subject; For the lead 4). Sessions 42 to 50 of the year, weeks 21 to 25; the year-end check is sessions 51 and 52. | Candidate: the class improvement chosen so its election and first step fit group 1's Application | The teacher or a card plays the pressure and the unkind part; no student plays the one who pushes or is left out. No real friends are named. Tobacco and body-safety topics are health's (health grade 4 unit 12). RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
200: | 12 | 4 | Thinking it through. *How do I make a choice that is fair to the people it touches?* | 12 | g4-rds-ask-questions; g4-rds-check-facts; g4-rds-good-for-us; g4-rds-solve-problems; g4-rds-think-ahead; g4-rds-who-is-affected; g4-sa-skills-grow; g4-sa-what-interests-me |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e4-reasons-evidence, the reasons and evidence a speaker gives for a point | Three groups, with a twelfth session as a second Application for group 2 (the moved session, Days). A two-week practice record starts on session 1 (the carry-out cue). Group 1, questions and claims: a partner interviewed with open questions about a choice the class faces, and how the answers changed the student's idea (RD1); a classmate's claim ("this snack gives you energy") checked against facts from a second source, and the choice made on what the facts show (RD2); a choice's results predicted for oneself and a second person, then compared with what happened in the story (RD4). Group 2, solve and map: a school problem solved by gathering two groups' views (younger students, lunch staff) before choosing and testing a solution, the second Application session given to the gathering (RD3); who a school choice touches mapped, how each group would see it, and the rule or group it answers to (RD7); a choice where one's own well-being and a friend's pull apart (stay up for the friend's call, or sleep before the game), picked with a reason (RD6). Group 3, interests and growth: a purpose added to the private interest log that connects to other people, and who might share the interest (SA9); the student's practice record compared with a story character's, and how another person's feedback changed the practice, never belief alone (SA8). Retrieval: the grade 3 rows (units 2, 5 and 6) in the openings; health grade 4 units 9 and 11 (deciding with help, the source check) open RD6 and RD2 as retrieval where the school runs health. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (a claim checked against a second source; two groups' views on one choice; results for a second person) and a partner interview rehearsed from an unseen choice card; sampled again by grade 5's mid-year check. Carry it out: "who else does it touch?" before class choices. Family activity: interview a family member about a family choice. Tools: stop-think-choose, the problem size meter, the "who does it touch?" circle, a two-source claim card, the practice record, the private log. Curio Class writes the claim cards and their two sources, the interview frame, the cases and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6, CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7, CASEL.Self-Awareness.8, CASEL.Self-Awareness.9. | Last because it gathers the year's second-person views into choices, and hands every grade 4 row on to grade 5. Eight sub-skills, three groups and 12 sessions: it takes the session grades 4 moved (Days), given to gathering two groups' views, the heaviest task of the year. ELA grade 4 unit 3 (course days 81 to 119) taught reasons and evidence first; health grade 4 units 9 and 11 come before (Cross-subject). Sessions 53 to 64 of the year, weeks 27 to 32. | Candidate: the two groups' views gathered by the teacher's arranged short visit or written questions, with a classroom-only version where a visit cannot be arranged | The snack claim checks a source, not a health claim's truth; nutrition is health's (health grade 4 unit 11). The log stays with the student, checked only that the steps were followed. Visits to other groups are arranged by the teacher. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
201: | 13 | 5 | Changes ahead. *How can I stay steady when something big is about to change?* | 10 | g5-sa-feel-think-link; g5-sa-name-feelings; g5-sm-big-feelings; g5-sm-calm-down; g5-soc-read-situation | CASEL.Self-Awareness.3; CASEL.Self-Awareness.5; CASEL.Self-Management.1; CASEL.Self-Management.2; CASEL.Social Awareness.7 | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e5-theme-response, how characters respond to a challenge | Two groups. Group 1, naming the change: mixed feelings about a coming change named and rated for a story character (moving to middle school), two feelings that pull opposite ways, and why naming both helps (SA3); a think-feel-do chain for a character whose friends say middle school is scary, and a balanced thought that keeps to the facts (SA5). Group 2, steady through change: an invented schedule change on a test day, two responses listed and one picked, then the calm-down steps and the chosen response rehearsed in a short scene with a partner, and what reacting would have cost (SM1); a calm-down plan for a time of change, two tools tested in a timed practice (two minutes each on a sand timer), and the one that worked kept with a reason (SM2); what a new situation will ask (a middle school visit, a new team), the chances it offers and what missing them would cost (SO7). Grade 5 is the owner year: each row adds the pressure of change. Retrieval: the grade 4 rows (units 7 and 10) in the openings; health grade 5 unit 15's habit list (the same weeks) names the calm tools, which this unit teaches. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (two opposite feelings and their clues; a balanced thought; a new situation's chance and its cost) and the calm-down steps with a chosen response rehearsed from an unseen card. Carry it out: "stop, breathe, name it, choose" before a schedule change. Family activity: test two calm tools at home and keep the one that worked. Tools: the feelings faces chart, the chain sheet, the calm-down plan card, sand timers. Curio Class writes the cases, the middle-school-visit scene and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $9 printing and $6 once for six sand timers where the room has none. | First because the year's angle, ready for change, starts from feelings and calm, which every later grade 5 rehearsal uses. Five sub-skills, two groups and 10 sessions. It runs beside ELA grade 5 unit 1 and health grade 5 unit 15 (weeks 1 to 4) (Cross-subject). Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: the timed practice kept to two tools and two minutes each so the timer is a pace cue, not a race | No student is asked about their own coming change or worries; cases are characters'. No clinical words; mental health topics are health's. Body tools beside desks with room to reach. If a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps. Agent reads: sensitivity (no own move or loss asked); safety (body tools). |
