p-steps ("pick the right helper") open RS8 as retrieval where the school runs health, and where it does not this unit teaches the asking from the meter. End-of-unit evidence: an unseen scene page (a problem sized and its helper; a fair solution from two; a situation's three questions answered) and a four-part I-statement rehearsed from an unseen card, the teacher playing the other part. Carry it out: "say back first" in every class talk. Family activity: use the four-part I-statement once at home on a small thing. Tools: the four-step talk turn card, the four-part I-statement frame, the four-level problem size meter, the trust sort cards, scene cards. Curio Class writes the three cards, the problem deck, the cases and the page. Provisional cost for 30: about $10 printing. Begun here, owned in grade 5 (the band's owner year; the grade 5 unit that owns each is in the Seams table): CASEL.Relationship Skills.1, CASEL.Relationship Skills.2, CASEL.Relationship Skills.5, CASEL.Relationship Skills.7, CASEL.Relationship Skills.8, CASEL.Social Awareness.7. | After unit 3 because talking out a disagreement uses reading the other side; it grows the talk turn card, I-statement and meter that units 5 and 6 use. Six sub-skills, mostly rehearsed by doing, in two groups of three with two Fluency sessions each, so 10 sessions. ELA grade 3 unit 1 set the talk norms earlier; health grade 3 unit 3 (weeks 10 to 14) taught which helper fits which problem; health grade 3 unit 4 (weeks 17 to 21) teaches its I-statement in the same weeks (Cross-subject; For the lead 4). Sessions 33 to 42 of the year, weeks 17 to 21. | Candidate: the I-statement and listening wording settled with health grade 3 unit 4 before either unit plan is written (For the lead 4) | No real friends are named. The teacher plays any upset or hurt part. RS8: if a student tells of harm or risk, health's steps, named because asking for help can bring one. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
