165: g4-soc-fair-rules | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.6 | Unit 1, Team rules we make. Teams review invented club rules from two sides, a newcomer's and a longtime member's, with the three fairness questions, and agree a version both could accept. Grade 4 adds the second view. Teacher note: invented class, club or game rules only; unfair rules of the past wait for grade 5; no current dispute.
166: g4-rel-teamwork | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.4 | Unit 1, Team rules we make. Teams write their own working agreement (how we decide, how we share jobs) and use it on a group build; the student names how a teammate saw a problem differently and how the team used both ideas. The grade 4 turn: a teammate's angle shapes the plan.
167: g4-rds-careful-thinking | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 | Unit 2, Plans and steps. The student reviews a team plan with stop-think-choose, asks a teammate where careful thinking caught a mistake, and writes one place outside school where it would help. Grade 4 adds a partner who points out what the thinking caught.
168: g4-sa-i-can-try | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 | Unit 2, Plans and steps. For two invented characters with the same hard goal, the student plans two different paths on the goal steps card, each with a helper, and says why one path fits each character better. This year two characters, two paths: what fits one person may not fit another. The course never records a student's own answer.
169: g4-sm-stay-on-task | ~CASEL.Self-Management.3 | Unit 2, Plans and steps. Across a two-week team project the student uses the focus plan and its try-again step (try another way, then ask) and checks in with a partner who uses theirs; they compare what pulls each of them off task or stalls them on a hard step, and swap one tip. Grade 4 adds the partner: two plans compared and a tip traded.
170: g4-sm-set-goal | ~CASEL.Self-Management.4 | Unit 2, Plans and steps. With a partner the student sets a shared goal on the goal steps card, each with their own steps, and changes a step after hearing what blocks the partner. A second person's block now shapes the plan.
171: g4-sm-plan-and-tidy | ~CASEL.Self-Management.5 | Unit 2, Plans and steps. The team splits a project into a timeline with deadlines and who does what; the student keeps their own materials list, and at review compares how a teammate planned the same job. Grade 4 adds the team: the plan holds other people's parts and deadlines.
172: g4-sm-step-forward | ~CASEL.Self-Management.6 | Unit 2, Plans and steps. The student proposes a first step for a team project, asks a teammate's view before starting, and starts it, saying how the other view changed the start. New in grade 4: a teammate's view shapes the first step.
173: g4-sa-who-i-am | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.1 | Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student compares two invented characters' identity webs, finds what they share and what differs, and may add to their own private web. Grade 4 sets one web beside another. Teacher note: invented character first; a student may make up their own; family and culture are never asked about by name in a check.
174: g4-sa-my-strengths | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.2 | Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student finds a strength a story character brings from their culture or language, and a strength a teammate saw in them, and compares the two views of a strength. Grade 4 adds how another person sees a strength. Teacher note: home languages and culture are welcome when offered, never asked about.
175: g4-soc-notice-good | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.2 | Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student gives specific feedback that names a strength the classmate may not see in themselves, and asks how they see it. The grade 4 turn: the classmate's own view of the strength is asked for.
176: g4-soc-their-view | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.1 | Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student retells a school scene from two characters' points of view with the shoes switch, and writes what each misunderstood about the other. The two sides now meet in one write-up.
177: g4-sa-snap-judgments | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 | Unit 3, Many ways to be. The student checks snap judgments in a case where two characters judge a third differently, finds what each assumed, and uses stop-think-choose to decide what to find out. Grade 4 adds a second judge: two characters, two guesses. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group.
178: g4-rel-different-ways | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.3 | Unit 3, Many ways to be. In a project with a story partner whose customs differ, the student plans how to blend two ways of doing a task and asks how the partner sees the blend. This year the partner's view of the blend counts. Teacher note: no student is asked to explain their own culture; customs come from stories and invented partners.
179: g4-sa-name-feelings | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.3 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student names and rates feelings for two characters in one team scene on the feelings faces scale and explains how the same event made them feel differently. Grade 4 adds a second character: one event, two feelings. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded.
180: g4-sa-feel-think-link | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.5 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student fills two think-feel-do chains for two characters after the same event (a team loses) and shows how different thoughts led to different feelings and actions. Grade 4 runs the chain for two people at once. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud.
181: g4-sm-big-feelings | ~CASEL.Self-Management.1 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. In a team scene where two characters' strong feelings collide, the student uses the calm-down steps and says how their response lands on the teammate. New in grade 4: the response is judged by how it lands on someone else.
182: g4-sm-calm-down | ~CASEL.Self-Management.2 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student builds a calm-down plan for a team moment (a project falling behind) and coaches a partner through theirs with the cue word, noticing which tool works for whom. Grade 4 adds a partner: the student coaches someone else's plan. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing; mental health topics are health's. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
183: g4-soc-care-when-struggle | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.3 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student uses the care steps for a struggling teammate and adapts when the teammate wants something different from what the student would want. Grade 4 adds the other person's wish, which may differ from the student's own. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
184: g4-soc-read-feelings | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.4 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student reads the clues of two people in a scene on the feelings faces chart and checks in with one in a way that fits that person (quietly, later, with a question). Grade 4 adds the fit: the check-in suits the other person, not the student.
185: g4-soc-thankful | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.5 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The team writes thanks to a person who helped the project, from that person's side: what helping cost them and what it made possible. New this year: thanks written from the helper's side.
186: g4-soc-why-rules | ~CASEL.Social Awareness.8 | Unit 4, Feelings at work. The student traces how one school or town system (recess rules, a bike lane) shapes what two different people do, such as a student and a crossing guard, and why each might see it differently. Grade 4 adds a second person: one system seen from two places in it.
187: g4-rel-friendships | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.2 | Unit 5, Leading and following. In a team case where two friends land in different groups, the student repairs the hurt with own it, sorry and a plan, and hears the friend's side first. The student rehearses the repair talk aloud with a partner playing the friend, using the I-statement frame. Grade 4 adds the friend's side: it is heard before the sorry. Teacher note: no real friends are named.
188: g4-rel-settle-disagreements | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.5 | Unit 5, Leading and following. With a partner, the student works a team disagreement where both sides have good reasons: each says the other's view first, then they use the I-statement frame and find a solution that meets both. Grade 4 adds the other side's view, said before one's own. The teacher plays any upset part.
189: g4-rel-ask-offer-help | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 | Unit 5, Leading and following. The student notices when an invented teammate needs help and offers it without taking over, and sizes a team problem on the problem size meter to know when to bring in a trusted adult. The student rehearses the offer aloud with a partner playing the teammate, then the ask with the partner playing the adult. Grade 4 adds the teammate: help offered so the other person keeps the job. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
190: g4-rel-lead-follow | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.7 | Unit 5, Leading and following. The student leads a team task with the three leading steps, then follows, and reflects on how leader and follower saw the same decision. This year the leader's and the follower's views of one decision are compared.
191: g4-rel-say-no | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.6 | Unit 5, Leading and following. The student rehearses the refusal steps when a card character urges the whole group to break a rule (skip the hard part, blame another team), and holds the no while keeping the friendship. Grade 4 adds the group: the no is held while the friendship is kept. Teacher note: the teacher or a card plays the pressure; no student plays the one who pushes; substance and body-safety topics are health's.
192: g4-rel-stand-up | ~CASEL.Relationship Skills.9 | Unit 5, Leading and following. The student plans how a leader and how a follower could each speak up when a teammate is left out, choosing from the upstander options for each role. The student then rehearses the leader's words and the follower's words with a partner, using the I-statement frame, while the teacher or a card plays the unkind act. Grade 4 adds the role: what a leader can say differs from what a follower can. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course.
193: g4-sm-have-a-say | ~CASEL.Self-Management.7 | Unit 5, Leading and following. The class elects a team to carry out a class improvement, and the student weighs a classmate's different proposal before voting. Grade 4 adds a classmate's different proposal, weighed before the vote.
194: g4-rds-good-for-us | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.6 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student weighs a choice where their own well-being and a friend's pull apart (stay up for the friend's call, or sleep before the game) and picks with a reason. New in grade 4: a friend's well-being and one's own pull apart.
195: g4-sa-what-interests-me | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student adds to the private interest log a purpose that connects to other people and names who might share the interest. Teacher note: the log stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content.
196: g4-rds-ask-questions | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.1 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student interviews a partner with open questions about a choice the class faces and writes how the partner's view changed their own idea. Grade 4 adds the interview: a partner's answers can change the student's idea.
197: g4-rds-check-facts | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student checks a claim a classmate makes (this snack gives you energy) against facts from a second source, and chooses on what the facts show. This year a second source is checked against a classmate's claim.
198: g4-rds-solve-problems | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.3 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The team solves a school problem by gathering two groups' views (younger students, lunch staff) before choosing and testing a solution. Grade 4 adds other groups' views before the choice.
199: g4-rds-think-ahead | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.4 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student predicts the results of a choice for themselves and for a second person, then compares the prediction with what happened in the story. Grade 4 adds the second person: results predicted for someone else too.
200: g4-rds-who-is-affected | ~CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.7 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student maps who a school choice touches and how each group would see it, then names the rule or group the choice must answer to. New in grade 4: how each group would see the choice.
201: g4-sa-skills-grow | ~CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 | Unit 6, Thinking it through. The student compares their practice record with a story character's and explains how feedback from another person changed the practice. Grade 4 adds another person's feedback as a cause of growth. Growth comes from practice, strategy and help, never belief alone.

