207: g5-sa-what-interests-me | CASEL.Self-Awareness.9 | Unit 2, What drives me. The student finds a purpose for an interest that costs something (practice time) and decides privately whether it is worth it. Owner year for 3 to 5: an interest weighed against what it costs. Teacher note: the log stays with the student; it is checked only that the steps were followed, never for its content.
208: g5-sa-i-can-try | CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 | Unit 2, What drives me. The student plans a stretch goal for a character facing a setback that costs something (a free afternoon), with two steps, a helper and a backup plan on the goal steps card. Owner year for 3 to 5: the plan carries a backup for a setback that costs something. The course never records a student's own answer.
209: g5-sa-skills-grow | CASEL.Self-Awareness.8 | Unit 2, What drives me. When a friend in a story says "you're just not good at it", the student uses a three-week practice record to explain the setback as a sign to change strategy or get help. Never belief alone. Owner year for 3 to 5: growth explained against someone else's put-down. Also carries AASL.V.D.2 (skill grows with practice and help), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL.
210: g5-sm-stay-on-task | CASEL.Self-Management.3 | Unit 2, What drives me. The student keeps on task through a long project when friends offer something more fun and when the next step gets hard, using the focus plan and a try-again step (try another way, then ask), and says what giving in would cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: focus held against friends' pull and through a hard step. Also carries AASL.V.D.1 (trying again when a task gets hard), placed in SEL by library (docs/courses/library/STANDARD.md section 4); it moves into the standard cell once scripts/progression.py accepts AASL ids for SEL.
211: g5-sm-set-goal | CASEL.Self-Management.4 | Unit 2, What drives me. The student sets a four-week goal on the goal steps card (it runs inside the unit and is judged at its end), tracks it weekly, and decides what to drop when two goals compete for time. Owner year for 3 to 5: a goal adjusted when two goals compete.
212: g5-sm-plan-and-tidy | CASEL.Self-Management.5 | Unit 2, What drives me. The student plans a multi-week project backward from a deadline and recovers when a step slips, saying what the slip cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: a plan built backward that survives a slip.
213: g5-rds-careful-thinking | CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.5 | Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student shows on an invented case how careful thinking held up when a group pushed for a fast choice, and what hurrying would have cost. Owner year for 3 to 5: careful thinking held against a group's hurry.
214: g5-sa-snap-judgments | CASEL.Self-Awareness.6 | Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student notices how a group can push a snap judgment (friends agree about a new student) and checks it anyway with the three steps and stop-think-choose. Owner year for 3 to 5: the check held when friends share the guess. Teacher note: invented cases only; the skill is noticing and checking one's own guesses, never blame of a group.
215: g5-soc-why-rules | CASEL.Social Awareness.8 | Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student traces how a school system and a town system (school rules, park signs and fines) shape what people do, and names a pressure that makes people follow or break them. Owner year for 3 to 5; media and online norms begin in grade 6.
216: g5-rds-check-facts | CASEL.Responsible Decision-Making.2 | Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student checks a claim like "everyone is doing it" against facts and numbers and chooses on the evidence even when friends lean the other way. Owner year for 3 to 5: the facts chosen over the crowd.
217: g5-soc-fair-rules | CASEL.Social Awareness.6 | Unit 3, Who shapes what we do. The student uses the three fairness questions on a norm from settled history that was unfair and was changed: school segregation, struck down by the Supreme Court in 1954, is the model case. They name what changing it cost the people who did. Owner year for 3 to 5: the first year past playground fairness. Teacher note: settled facts the courts have ruled on, taught with dignity; the history is social studies' unit; current political disputes are not SEL content.
