docs/courses/physical-education-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:271:| calm-steps | PE K to 2 (g1-calm-steps), in SEL's words. Not new: SEL grades 3 to 5 grows the calm-down steps to four (stop, breathe, name it, choose; g3-sm-calm-down, g3-sm-big-feelings) and PE's card uses those four words | Unit 6 (g3-calm: the method that works best for me, used after a miss with a tool) | Units 11 (g4-with-others), 24 (g5-review-experiences: what the calm steps did); goes on to grade 7 |
docs/courses/physical-education-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:285:| 6 | 3 | Strike with tools. *How do I hit a ball with a bat or a paddle, and which calming method works best for me when I miss?* | 8 | g3-strike-tools; g3-calm | SHAPE.S1.5.11; SHAPE.S1.5.16; SHAPE.S4.5.9 | Cues: "side to the target, eyes on the ball, swing through"; SEL's four calm-down steps "stop, breathe, name it, choose". Settings: off a tee, a self-toss, a gentle partner toss; one batter per space | Hit with a long-handled tool (plastic bat, hockey-style stick) in several tasks (S1.5.11) and a short-handled tool (paddle, racket) in several tasks (S1.5.16), off a tee, a self-toss and a gentle partner toss; name which calming methods work best during activity (a slow breath, counting, a drink of water, stepping out for a minute with a role) and use one after a miss (S4.5.9). The calm-down steps are SEL's for grades 3 to 5 (g3-sm-calm-down), in SEL's words; the unit opens them as a retrieval task and teaches choosing and using one while moving, on PE's calm-steps card. Opens also with a retrieval task on K to 2 grade 2 unit 19's long-tool striking. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: contacts a gently tossed ball three of five times with a swing through, with three paddle hits to a wall as the last Fluency day's closure watch, carried; knowledge task: names the calming method that works best for them and shows it; conduct: swing space and the batter's line kept, S3.5.4 review), 1 respond day | Striking a tossed ball needs the tracking of unit 2's catches; it sits before the year-end check so the check samples standard 4; striking misses often, which makes the calming method real | Candidate: a private calm card where each child circles their best method | Swing space cleared; one batter per space; the line stands behind a safe distance; foam balls. Emotions: calming is offered, never a punishment; a child may step out for a reset with a role; the teacher page names the school counselor. Sensitivity read sources: the national child mental health center's pages on children's self-calming. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $60 (15 short rackets; reuses K to 2's paddles, bats and foam balls) |
docs/courses/physical-education-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:290:| 11 | 4 | Attack together. *How does my team move the ball into the other team's area, and how do we decide who does what so everyone is part of it?* | 11 | g4-attack-ideas; g4-team-roles; g4-with-others | SHAPE.S2.5.2; SHAPE.S3.5.1; SHAPE.S3.5.2; SHAPE.S4.5.2; SHAPE.S4.5.5 | Cues: "spread out, pass to open, move to space"; the tactics-question; team talk. Settings: three-on-three keep-away and end-zone games with no elimination, on several small courts | Explain how to attack where teams move into the other team's area: spread out, pass to open players, move to space (S2.5.2); explain how others see a situation, a player who is left out or a slower player (S3.5.1), and talk with others to decide who does what (S3.5.2); describe how favorite activities connect a child with others (S4.5.2) and spot difficulties a group faces when moving together, too little space, uneven skill, one player taking every turn, with a fix (S4.5.5). Calm steps return when a game gets heated (review). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 1's safe-movement tactics, unit 5's kindness in groups and SEL grade 4 unit 7's working agreement (weeks 1 to 5). Out: defending (grade 5). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: moves to open space and receives a pass in a three-on-three game, unit 10's pass cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge tasks: names two attacking moves in a game picture; says how the left-out player in a pictured game sees it (S3.5.1); one connection and one group difficulty with a fix; conduct: takes a role and decides with the group using team talk, S3.5.2), 1 respond day | After unit 10 because attacking is passing to open players; team roles and group difficulties are taught here because small-sided team play is where they first arise; it sits before the mid-year check so the check samples standards 3 and 4 | Candidate: a group plan card where the team writes who does what before a game | Teams made privately by the teacher; a more and a less competitive court offered; no scores posted. Pinnies reused. Cost: $0 |
docs/courses/physical-education-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:295:| 16 | 4 | Activities from around the world. *What do children play and dance in other places, and what can we learn about each activity from where it comes from?* | 7 | g4-world-activities | SHAPE.S3.5.7 | Cues: each activity's one-line rule card; "watch, wait, search, race back" for Pilolo. Settings: a kicking game in small circles, a search-and-race game in small groups with no one out | Take part in movement activities from cultures around the world, describe each one and name the culture it comes from, on a world map (S3.5.7, in full: describe, not only name). Picks: jianzi, a shuttlecock-kicking game from China, played in small circles to keep the shuttlecock up with the feet (counting the group's kicks, no one out); and Pilolo, a search-and-race game from Ghana: one player hides small objects (sticks or stones in the game as described; beanbags here) while the others turn away, then on the call all search and race to bring one to the leader, in small groups with the hider's turn rotating and no one out (rules vary by source; the unit uses the version the agent confirms). Neither is used in any other PE, dance, music or SEL frame of K to 12. Readiness check on g1-world-games (K to 2 unit 14, grade 1) on day 73. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: five kicks of the shuttlecock alone or in a circle with an inside-foot kick, the cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge task: describes one activity, how it is played and where it comes from; conduct: the group counts every member's kicks and invites a player in, S3.5.3 review), 1 respond day | Last in grade 4: it needs the foot skills of unit 5 and the scanning and safe traveling of unit 1; social studies has taught the world map (grade 3 unit 1) and teaches regional cultures beside it (Cross-subject) | Candidate: a family-shared game, used only with family consent and where its source says it is open to be taught | Cultural content: communities named are Chinese and Ghanaian. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: jianzi at an encyclopedia entry and a university course collection on Chinese shuttlecock; Pilolo at an encyclopedia entry (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pilolo) and a US state early learning activity page (https://gelds.decal.ga.gov/Activity/Detail/2520), both opened by the expert read, neither a Ghanaian source, so Pilolo stays a candidate until the agent confirms it at a Ghanaian cultural or education institution's page (For the lead 7); for jianzi the agent studies a Chinese cultural or education institution's page first. Hazards and controls: Pilolo's race runs in marked lanes in one direction, objects placed flat on the floor, no diving for them; shuttlecocks kicked inside small circles away from faces. Swap allowed. Cost: $25 (30 shuttlecocks; beanbags reused) |
docs/courses/physical-education-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:297:| 18 | 5 | Invasion games. *How does my team attack and defend in a small game, and how do we settle it when we disagree about a call?* | 13 | g5-invasion-skills; g5-defend-ideas; g5-conflict-solve | SHAPE.S2.5.3; SHAPE.S3.5.5 | Cues: unit 10's pass and send cues on the card; "between the player and the goal, close the space, eyes on the ball"; team talk. Settings: three-on-three soccer-style and basketball-style games with no elimination | Passing, dribbling with hands and feet, and sending to a target used in three-on-three invasion games: used in new settings, SHAPE.S1.5.17 and SHAPE.S1.5.21 (owned in grade 4, unit 10) and SHAPE.S1.5.18, SHAPE.S1.5.19 and SHAPE.S1.5.20 (owned in grade 3, unit 5); explain how to defend: stay between the player and the goal, close the space, eyes on the ball (S2.5.3); work through problems alone, with a partner and in a small team, each taught and watched: alone, choosing and testing a safe fix for an equipment or space problem; with a partner, agreeing and testing a fair turn-taking rule; in a small team, settling a disagreement over a rule or a call with calm steps and team talk (S3.5.5, in full). Opens with retrieval tasks on unit 11's attacking moves and team roles, unit 10's passing and SEL grade 4 unit 11's settle-disagreement steps (Cross-subject). Ends: 2 check days (skill task: a pass to an open teammate and a dribble-and-send in a game, unit 10's cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge task: names two defending moves in a game picture; conduct: resolves a disagreement over a call in a real game with team talk, or in a scripted scene with invented players, never a child playing a part against another, with the alone and partner fixes watched on practice days and carried, S3.5.5), 1 respond day | Invasion games use every grade 4 sending skill and unit 11's attacking ideas, so defending comes now; games are where disagreements over calls happen | Candidate: the team writes its own two-sentence disagreement rule | Teams made privately; a more and a less competitive court; no scores posted; foam balls; no body contact rule. Cost: $0 (pinnies, balls and cones reused) |
