docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:289:| 3 | 3 | Helpers and careful choices. *Which helper fits which problem, and when do I stop and think before I choose?* | 5 | g3-s-which-helper; g3-phw-find-resources; g3-s-decide-careful; g3-s-stay-safe-places; g3-aod-medicine-rules | NHES.S3.5.1; NHES.S3.5.2; NHES.S5.5.1 | Accessing help (standard 3): help-steps, the second step (pick the right helper and the right words); deciding (standard 5): decision-steps, notice a careful choice | Day 1: which adult or resource fits which situation (a scraped knee, a worry, a lost item, a question about food), picked and said in the help-step words (NHES.S3.5.1); retrieval of g2-s-find-school-helpers (grade 2 unit 13). Day 2: wellness resources at home, at school and in the community (nurse, clinic, library, a trusted website a teacher names), and each student builds a one-page "where to go" list with a family or school adult (NHES.S3.5.2); medicines only as directed by a trusted adult and first plain facts on alcohol: it is not for children and why (~NHES.S3.5.1 practice); retrieval of g2-phw-doctor-nurse-visit. Day 3: situations that call for careful thinking before choosing (a dare, a medicine, a stranger online, a risky play spot), sorted from easy choices (NHES.S5.5.1). Day 4: safety at home, in a car, near water, on a bike and online in its simplest form (ask before you click, no private details): students decide for a set of cases with the reason, without the card (~NHES.S5.5.1 application); emergency rule kept from grade 2: get an adult first; if no adult can help, call 911; retrieval of g2-s-outdoor-and-home-safety. Day 5: end-of-unit check: unseen cases, pick the right helper and the words; spot the careful choice; give the rule and reason for a place. Out: refusal steps (grade 4), alcohol effects beyond "not for children". Serves HECAT.S.HBO1, HBO3, HBO4, HBO5, HBO6, HBO7; PHW.HBO5; AOD.HBO1, HBO2, HBO3. Curio Class writes: helper situation cards, "where to go" sheet, careful-or-easy sort, place cases, a printed invented screen. Cost: $3 printing | The getting-help unit of grade 3 in a non-sensitive place, so units 6 and 7 can be left out, and before the mid-year check so it is sampled. After unit 2 because careful choices meet the pushes unit 2 named. SEL grade 3's ask-for-help row leans on it (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT S module 3-5 read may add fire escape plans and pedestrian safety; one case each inside day 4 | Medicine and alcohol: pictures and invented labels only; never test knowledge of home medicines; a child who lives with someone who drinks is never made to feel blame. The "where to go" list stays with the student. No real number dialed. Agent reads: sensitivity (safety sources named in the brief) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:290:| 4 | 3 | Saying it, hearing it, and standing up. *How do I say what I need, really listen, and help when someone is bullied?* | 5 | g3-meh-feelings-say; g3-meh-listening; g3-v-bullying; g3-v-my-limits-and-yours | NHES.S4.5.1; NHES.S4.5.2 | Communicating (standard 4): the grade 3 I-statement and the listening steps; boundary steps and conflict steps returned | Day 1: say clearly what I think and need, in a way that helps health: "I feel ___ when ___ because ___. Please ___.", modeled by the teacher on scripts about a friend, a group and a teacher (NHES.S4.5.1); retrieval of g2-meh-feelings-calm-and-talk (grade 2 unit 14). Day 2: careful listening in several places (class, a game, at home, on a call): look, wait, ask one question, say back what you heard; students rate their listening on a card (NHES.S4.5.2). Day 3: bullying is repeated unkind acts that hurt; the roles (the one bullied, the one bullying, bystanders who can help); an upstander line and telling an adult, with a card playing the unkind line (~NHES.S4.5.2 practice: listening to the one hurt); retrieval of g2-v-settle-it-with-words. Day 4: stating a limit (space, belongings, tickling, shared photos) and respecting others' limits with the boundary steps (~NHES.S4.5.4 practice, owned in grade 4 unit 12); retrieval of g2-v-my-limits-say-stop; the I-statement heard from a third of the class. Day 5: end-of-unit check: unseen scripts, write or say the I-statement for a character, the listening steps for a scene, an upstander line; the I-statement heard from the rest of the class. Out: conflict resolution in full (grade 5), online bullying (grade 6). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO1, HBO2, HBO7, HBO8; V.HBO1, HBO3. Curio Class writes: scripts, step cards, bullying scenes with characters varied in family, ability and culture. Cost: $3 printing | After the mid-year check, when the class knows the routine; the communication unit of grade 3, before unit 6, which uses the boundary steps. SEL grade 3 unit 5 rehearses the upstander plan and SEL unit 1 uses the same feelings words (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT MEH and V module 3-5 reads may add "ways to show care when a friend is upset"; it fits inside day 2 | No child is asked about being bullied or about feelings at home; scenes are characters'. A child being bullied now is never named in a scene; the teacher follows the school's bullying policy if a student tells. No role play of bullying. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:291:| 5 | 3 | Strengths and a goal. *What strength can I grow, and is my goal best done alone or together?* | 3 | g3-phw-grow-strengths; g3-phw-set-goal; g3-phw-goal-alone-or-together | NHES.S1.5.1; NHES.S6.5.1; NHES.S6.5.2 | Setting goals (standard 6): goal-plan returned (say the goal and why) | Day 1: how to grow a strength I already have that keeps me healthy (a calm routine, a sport skill, asking for help) by practicing it and finding a helper (NHES.S1.5.1); one health goal in a sentence from the safe list (sleep, water, handwashing, time outside, screen breaks, a kind act, asking for help) and how it helps health (NHES.S6.5.1); retrieval of g2-phw-why-health-matters and g2-phw-goal-do-it (grade 2 units 12 and 15). Day 2: whether a goal is best done alone, with a helper or as a team (walking to school with friends, a family sleep routine), sorted with a reason (NHES.S6.5.2), with the help-steps' helper step. Day 3: end-of-unit check: the student's own goal sentence with its reason and its alone-or-together choice, or a character's, scored on the goal-plan steps; one unseen sort item. Out: a full plan with resources and a check (grade 4). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2, HBO7. Curio Class writes: strength cards, goal frame sheet, alone-or-together sort. Cost: $2 printing | Last non-sensitive unit of grade 3, so the goal sentence hands on to grade 4 unit 8 as the first plan, and it closes the year after the communication unit. SEL grade 3 unit 6 sets a school goal on its own card; health's goals stay on the safe health list (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 3-5 read may add "name a person who can help with a goal"; inside day 2 | Goals from the safe list only, never weight, body or food limits; a character's goal is always an option; the goal stays with the student. Strengths are never body traits. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:294:| 8 | 4 | A plan for a goal. *What does a goal plan need, and what could help or block it?* | 4 | g4-phw-goal-plan; g4-phw-goal-helps-and-blocks; g4-pa-warm-up | NHES.S6.5.3; NHES.S6.5.4 | Setting goals (standard 6): goal-plan with actions, resources and a check | Day 1: a goal plan with actions, resources and a way to check progress, modeled on a character's plan; each student writes a one-week plan for a safe-list goal, and the first plan can build a strength from grade 3 (NHES.S6.5.3, ~NHES.S1.5.1 practice); retrieval of g3-phw-set-goal and g3-phw-grow-strengths (grade 3 unit 5). Day 2: what helps and what blocks progress (time, a friend, a space) and one way around a block, in pairs (NHES.S6.5.4); retrieval of g3-phw-goal-alone-or-together. Day 3: warm up, cool down, drink water and stop when hurt, then plan an activity week that supports growth, the goal plan applied in the physical activity topic without the card (~NHES.S7.5.2 and ~NHES.S6.5.3 practice; shared with PE, health's part is the plan); retrieval of g3-pa-active-and-invite (grade 3 unit 1). Day 4: end-of-unit check: the student's own plan (or a character's) scored on the plan steps, with one unseen block-and-way-around item. Out: tracking the plan over weeks (grade 5). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO1, HBO2; PA.HBO2, HBO3, HBO5, HBO6. Curio Class writes: plan sheet, helps-and-blocks cards, activity week sheet. Cost: $3 printing | First in grade 4 because SEL grade 4 unit 2 leans on health's goal plan (Cross-subject), and the plan carries straight on from grade 3 unit 5 the year before | Candidate: HECAT PA module 3-5 read may add "choose activities you enjoy"; one line in day 3 | Goals from the safe list only, never weight, body or food limits; plans stay with the student. Active day: a short warm-up in clear space with five minutes for clean-up; a child who cannot run chooses another movement. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (active day) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:295:| 9 | 4 | Staying well and deciding with help. *How do people keep illness and injury away, and when does a decision need someone else?* | 5 | g4-phw-prevent-illness-injury; g4-phw-routines-and-conditions; g4-s-decide-with-help; g4-fn-compare-options | NHES.S1.5.3; NHES.S1.5.4; NHES.S5.5.2; NHES.S5.5.3 | Deciding (standard 5): decision-steps, ask if it needs help and compare options and results | Day 1: ways to make illness and injury less likely (vaccines, handwashing, food and water safety, rest, safety gear), sorted by which habit helps, with the one fact stated and sourced: germs spread by touch, air, food and water, and vaccines teach the body to defend itself (NHES.S1.5.3, both illness and injury); retrieval of g3-phw-habits-look-closely (grade 3 unit 1). Day 2: healthy routines, including how people care for a long-term condition such as asthma, an allergy or diabetes, on a character's day; each student shows the steps of one taught school routine (handwashing, a water break, a warm-up) on a teacher record (NHES.S1.5.4); retrieval of g3-phw-find-resources (grade 3 unit 3). Day 3: whether a health decision needs someone's help or a team (a medicine, an online question, a friend in trouble), sorted by who should help (NHES.S5.5.2); retrieval of g3-s-decide-careful. Day 4: compare options and what each is likely to lead to on an everyday food choice and a safety choice, in a two-column "if I do this, then ..." chart (NHES.S5.5.3). Day 5: end-of-unit check: unseen cases, the habit that prevents an illness or injury and why, who should help with a decision, two options and their likely results; the routine record is carried into the unit judgment for NHES.S1.5.4. Out: how vaccines work in the body, treatment of any condition. Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO5, HBO8, HBO9; S.HBO5; FN.HBO8, HBO10. Facts and sources: germs and vaccines (CDC), school health conditions (CDC). Curio Class writes: illness-and-injury sort, a character's day with a condition, decide-with-help sort, if-then chart. Cost: $3 printing | After unit 8 so the year opens with the plan SEL leans on; before unit 10, whose emergency day uses the decide-with-help sort. Science grade 4 teaches body structures later, so health states its germ fact itself with its source (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PHW module 3-5 read may add "hearing and vision checks" and "food safety at home"; one line each inside day 1 | Conditions: never ask about conditions in the room; a condition is never a blame or a weakness; a student with asthma, an allergy or diabetes is never asked to explain theirs. Pictures only of inhalers and injectors. Vaccines: a plain fact, no illness stories, no debate staged; a family's choices are never discussed. Agent reads: sensitivity (conditions sources named in the brief) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:296:| 10 | 4 | Help with feelings and emergencies. *How do I ask for help with a worry, help a friend who asks, and get help in an emergency?* | 4 | g4-meh-calm-stress; g4-meh-help-with-feelings; g4-s-get-help-emergency | NHES.S4.5.3 | Communicating (standard 4): ask for and give help, with the help-steps and the I-statement | Day 1: signs of stress and worry, then pick a calm tool on the one calm-down card shared with SEL (breath, a walk, talking), then tell (~NHES.S4.5.1 practice, owned in grade 3 unit 4); ask for help and give it: pick an adult, use the I-statement, ask for one thing, thank them, modeled by the teacher (NHES.S4.5.3); retrieval of g3-meh-feelings-say (grade 3 unit 4) and g3-s-which-helper (grade 3 unit 3). Day 2: pairs rehearse the ask, and how to be the one who listens when a friend asks, on scripts (NHES.S4.5.3, the giving half); the help line named in plain words for families. Day 3: an emergency (what counts as one), how an adult calls for help, and how a child gives a name and a place; get an adult first; if no adult can help, call 911, practiced on a paper phone without a real call (~NHES.S4.5.3 and ~NHES.S3.5.1 practice); the ask heard from a third of the class. Day 4: end-of-unit check: unseen scripts, write or say the ask for a character and a listening reply for a friend, what counts as an emergency and what to say; the ask heard from the rest. Out: stress as an illness, any treatment talk. Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO4, HBO5; S.HBO7. Curio Class writes: stress-sign cards, ask-and-give scripts, emergency script, paper phone. Cost: $3 printing | The getting-help unit of grade 4 in a non-sensitive place, so units 13 and 14 can be left out; the mental health help-seeking outcome of the band is taught here (STANDARD.md section 4 key). Before the mid-year check. SEL grade 4 unit 4 leans on its stress day and SEL unit 5 on its ask (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT MEH and S module 3-5 reads may add "tell an adult if a friend talks about being hurt"; kept to telling an adult | No student is asked about their own worries or stress; scenes are characters'. A calm tool is never a punishment. The emergency call is practiced on a paper phone only, never a real number dialed; emergency scenes stay calm, with helpers shown; never ask a child to retell an emergency. Agent reads: sensitivity (safety sources named in the brief); safety (emergency script) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:297:| 11 | 4 | Influences and claims. *How do the same things affect people differently, and how do I know a health claim can be trusted?* | 4 | g4-phw-how-things-affect-us; g4-meh-same-thing-different-people; g4-fn-food-claims-source-check | NHES.S2.5.2; NHES.S2.5.3; NHES.S3.5.3 | Analyzing influences (standard 2): influence check on me and others; accessing information (standard 3): the source check introduced | Day 1: how influences affect my health and others' health with a real case (screen time and sleep, a friend's choice and mine), and picking one influence to counter with a habit (NHES.S2.5.2, own and others'); one factor can affect people and communities in different ways (a hot day with or without shade, a snack ad, a park nearby), and the reason is circumstance, not a person's worth (NHES.S2.5.3, people and communities); retrieval of g3-phw-influences-on-health (grade 3 unit 2). Day 2: the source check introduced and modeled (who made it, what they want, is there evidence, does an adult I trust agree) on a snack ad and a drink claim; retrieval of g3-fn-added-sugar-drinks (grade 3 unit 2). Day 3: pairs run the source check on a health product label and a flyer for a health service, then alone on a fact sheet, so facts, products and services are all judged (NHES.S3.5.3). Day 4: end-of-unit check: unseen cases, how one influence affects two characters and why, and the source check on a new claim with a reason to trust it or not. Out: searching on a device (grade 5 with an adult). Serves HECAT.PHW.HBO6; MEH.HBO7; FN.HBO8. Curio Class writes: two community cases, an invented snack ad, drink claim, label and service flyer, a fact sheet, the source-check card. Cost: $3 printing | After the mid-year check, in weeks 16 to 19, beside ELA grade 4 unit 3 (days 81 to 119), which teaches how an author uses reasons and evidence (RI.4.8). SEL grade 4 unit 6 checks a classmate's claim against a second source and leans on this row (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT FN module 3-5 read may add "read the serving line on a label"; one item in day 3, with no number work beyond comparing whole numbers | Community cases never rank neighborhoods or blame people for where they live. Ads, labels and flyers are invented, with no product or company names. No student describes their own screen time or sleep. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:298:| 12 | 4 | Limits, no, and speaking up. *How do I set a limit, say no to an offer, and speak up so others listen?* | 5 | g4-v-limits-and-respect; g4-t-saying-no; g4-v-speak-up-notice; g4-phw-advocacy-teams | NHES.S4.5.4; NHES.S4.5.5; NHES.S8.5.1; NHES.S8.5.2 | Communicating (standard 4): boundary steps and the refusal steps introduced; advocacy (standard 8): the speak-up plan introduced | Day 1: set personal limits and respect others' limits in friendships, games and online: ask, listen for a yes or no, accept it; consent for non-body activities (sharing a photo, borrowing, joining a game), with the boundary, listening and conflict steps (NHES.S4.5.4, both halves); retrieval of g3-v-my-limits-and-yours and g3-meh-listening (grade 3 unit 4). Day 2: the refusal steps (say no clearly, a short reason or none, suggest another thing, leave) modeled on an offered vape, a dare and a snack, with a card playing the offer; the norm said plainly, "most children your age have never tried a cigarette or a vape" (NHES.S4.5.5); retrieval of g3-t-tobacco-facts (grade 3 unit 2). Day 3: pairs rehearse the refusal on written scripts; notice when speaking up could help my own or another person's health or safety (someone left out, a friend ignoring a helmet rule), and name who to tell and what to say, the first two steps of the speak-up plan (NHES.S8.5.1, own or another's); retrieval of g3-v-bullying; the refusal heard from a third of the class. Day 4: how teamwork and clear communication make speaking up stronger: a small team writes one clear ask for more handwashing time or a shade structure, the whole speak-up plan applied (NHES.S8.5.2); retrieval of g3-pa-active-and-invite. Day 5: end-of-unit check: unseen scripts, the boundary steps and the refusal steps for a character, and a speak-up plan for a new case; the refusal heard from the rest. Out: delivering to a real audience (grade 5). Serves HECAT.V.HBO3, HBO7; MEH.HBO8; T.HBO1, HBO4; PHW.HBO5; PA.HBO7. Curio Class writes: limits scenes, refusal scripts with a card playing the offer, speak-up plan card, team ask sheet. Cost: $3 printing | The heaviest grade 4 unit (two new step cards). After unit 11 and before unit 13, which uses the limits steps. SEL grade 4 unit 5 leans on its refusal and speak-up rows (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT T and V module 3-5 reads may add "secondhand smoke and smoke-free places"; one line in day 2 | No student plays a person who pressures; a card plays the offer; scripted lines only. Grabbing, tickling and photo scenes are about characters. Pictures only for cigarettes and vapes. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:301:| 15 | 5 | Habits that last. *Can I keep a healthy habit going for weeks, and what does tracking it teach me?* | 4 | g5-meh-mind-habits; g5-pa-habit-practice; g5-phw-goal-track; g5-phw-goal-reflect | NHES.S7.5.2; NHES.S6.5.5; NHES.S6.5.6 | Practicing healthy behaviors (standard 7) and setting goals (standard 6): goal-plan with tracking and look back | Day 1: habits that support mental and emotional health (time outside, a friend, a creative thing, sleep) and the strengths that help me cope (~NHES.S7.5.2, ~NHES.S1.5.1 practice); each student picks a school habit from the safe list (a water break, a movement break, a calm minute) and starts a four-week log kept at school with a partner (NHES.S7.5.2); retrieval of g4-phw-goal-plan (grade 4 unit 8) and g4-meh-calm-stress (grade 4 unit 10). Day 2 (week 2): keep track of progress with the log and one check-in with the teacher as helper (NHES.S6.5.5); retrieval of g4-pa-warm-up; water before and after activity named from PE grade 5's food and drink row. Day 3 (week 3): the habit continues; reflect on how the goal setting is going and what resulted, and change one step; a missed day is a reason to change the plan, not a verdict on the student (NHES.S6.5.6). Day 4 (week 4): end-of-unit check: the student's own log, check-in record and written reflection, scored on the goal-plan steps and the telling, never on how many days were ticked; the teacher's record of each student carrying out the habit at school stands for NHES.S7.5.2 and NHES.S6.5.5. Out: a self-assessment of health (grade 6). Serves HECAT.MEH.HBO2, HBO9; PA.HBO1, HBO2, HBO3, HBO5; PHW.HBO2. Curio Class writes: a four-week log sheet, check-in card, reflection frame. Cost: $4 printing | First in grade 5 so the log runs four school weeks inside its own unit, and so it comes before SEL grade 5 unit 2's four-week goal, which leans on health's tracking row (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT PA and MEH module 3-5 reads may add "limit screen time before bed"; one safe-list choice | Goals and habits from the safe list only; the log stays at school and is never about weight, food or body; no grade on how well the habit went; a student who uses a wheelchair or cannot run picks a habit that fits. No student is asked about stress or feelings at home. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (movement breaks) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:303:| 17 | 5 | Choices under pressure, online and off. *How do I choose the safe option when friends, ads or strangers push?* | 5 | g5-aod-refuse-alcohol-drugs; g5-s-online-safety; g5-meh-handle-influences; g5-s-choose-safe-option; g5-meh-decision-look-back | NHES.S2.5.4; NHES.S5.5.4; NHES.S5.5.5 | Deciding (standard 5): the full decision steps; refusal steps returned; influences (standard 2): tools to deal with them | Day 1: facts about alcohol and other drugs that matter for a decision, including medicine misuse, and the norm said plainly, "most students your age do not drink alcohol or use drugs" (CDC, nearest age group, source and year at the expert read); the refusal steps returned with the card on a new scenario (~NHES.S4.5.5 practice); retrieval of g4-t-saying-no (grade 4 unit 12). Day 2: digital safety: keep private details private, refuse a request from a stranger, tell an adult about a message that feels wrong, on printed invented screens (~NHES.S4.5.5 practice); tools and resources to deal with things that affect wellness (screen limits with an adult, muting an account, a friend to back me up), a tool picked for each influence (NHES.S2.5.4); retrieval of g4-s-get-help-emergency and g4-meh-same-thing-different-people. Day 3: choose the option that supports health or safety using the full decision steps on a new scenario, modeled then in pairs, with the injury-prevention link (NHES.S5.5.4, ~NHES.S1.5.3 practice); retrieval of g4-fn-compare-options, g4-s-decide-with-help and g4-phw-prevent-illness-injury (grade 4 unit 9). Day 4: think about how a character's decision turned out for them and for others, what they would keep and change, without the card (NHES.S5.5.5, both halves). Day 5: end-of-unit check: unseen cases, the decision steps to a safe option, a refusal for a group offer, a tool for an online influence, and a look-back on a character's choice. Out: use figures (grade 6, where a national survey of the age exists). Serves HECAT.AOD.HBO1, HBO2, HBO3; S.HBO4, HBO5, HBO6; V.HBO5; MEH.HBO2, HBO5, HBO9. Curio Class writes: decision cases, refusal scripts with a card playing the offer, invented screens and messages, influence-tool cards. Cost: $3 printing | Before the mid-year check, a getting-help place for online safety (tell an adult). After unit 16 so facts are judged with the source check before they are used in a choice. SEL grade 5 units 4 and 6 lean on its refusal and look-back rows (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT AOD and S module 3-5 reads may add "medicine labels and doses" and "gaming chat"; one case each, no app named | Pictures and invented labels only for alcohol, drugs and medicines. No student is asked about drinking, drug use or online life at home; a child who lives with someone who drinks or uses drugs is never made to feel blame. No student plays a pressurer or a stranger; a card does. Agent reads: sensitivity (safety sources named in the brief) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:304:| 18 | 5 | Getting along. *How do we settle a conflict fairly and stay kind when we disagree?* | 3 | g5-v-conflict-steps; g5-meh-care-and-kindness | NHES.S4.5.6; NHES.S4.5.7 | Communicating (standard 4): conflict steps in full form, with listening and boundary steps | Day 1: stay out of, handle and settle conflicts: stop, calm down, each person says their side, find a fair way, or walk away and get help, modeled then rehearsed on written scripts for three conflicts (NHES.S4.5.6, all three: stay out of, handle, settle); retrieval of g4-v-limits-and-respect (grade 4 unit 12) and g4-meh-calm-stress (grade 4 unit 10); the conflict steps heard from half the class. Day 2: speak and act with care and kindness, including when we disagree (a kind word, including a new student, an apology), with the listening steps and the I-statement (NHES.S4.5.7); the conflict steps heard from the rest. Day 3: end-of-unit check: unseen scripts, the conflict steps for a character and a kind reply in a disagreement; valuing item on a character who walks away. Out: harassment and reporting (grade 6). Serves HECAT.V.HBO1, HBO2; MEH.HBO3, HBO7, HBO8. Curio Class writes: three conflict scripts, kindness scenes. Cost: $2 printing | After the mid-year check; the communication unit of grade 5. Its two rows are both skill rows practiced in the same scripts, so three days hold them. SEL grade 5 unit 4 leans on both rows (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT V module 3-5 read may add "online conflicts"; one script | Conflict scenes are characters'; no student re-enacts a real class conflict. Agent reads: sensitivity |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:305:| 19 | 5 | Speaking up for health. *How can our class speak up for a healthier, safer school, and who needs to hear it?* | 4 | g5-t-speak-up-smoke-free; g5-fn-help-others-eat-well; g5-s-speak-up-for-safety | NHES.S8.5.3; NHES.S8.5.4 | Advocacy (standard 8): the speak-up plan applied to a real audience | Day 1: name advocacy approaches (a poster, a letter, a team ask, a talk) for tobacco-free places and helping friends stay free of tobacco, and pick the audience (NHES.S8.5.3); helping others eat well as one approach: plan a class snack day with fruit and vegetable choices and a way to include every food culture (~NHES.S8.5.3 practice); retrieval of g4-phw-advocacy-teams and g4-t-saying-no (grade 4 unit 12), and of opinion writing with reasons from ELA grade 4 unit 3 (W.4.1). Day 2: each group drafts its message on a safety or wellness problem for a real audience (a short talk to a younger class, a note to the principal), on the speak-up plan; retrieval of g4-v-speak-up-notice; PE grade 5's food and drink row named in the snack-day plan. Day 3: written feedback from the teacher on each draft, revision and a rehearsal. Day 4: end-of-unit check: the message delivered to its real audience (the younger class or the principal visits, or the note is handed over and read aloud), scored on the speak-up plan steps and the delivery (NHES.S8.5.4), with one unseen item naming an approach and audience for a new case. Out: judging an advocacy campaign (grade 8). Serves HECAT.T.HBO3, HBO4; FN.HBO12; S.HBO8; V.HBO7. Curio Class writes: approach cards, speak-up plan sheet, feedback guide, snack-day plan sheet. Cost: $4 (printing, poster paper) | The last non-sensitive unit of the band, because it uses every advocacy step from grade 4 and the decision and refusal work of unit 17, and hands the speak-up plan on to grade 6. SEL grade 5 unit 5 leans on its speak-up-for-safety row (Cross-subject) | Candidate: HECAT T module 3-5 read may add "tobacco-free laws at school"; one line in day 1 | The snack day is a plan; if the school holds it, it follows the school's food and allergy rules and no family is asked to pay or bring food. Families' foods and budgets are never ranked. A student who does not speak delivers in writing or with a partner. Agent reads: sensitivity (food sources named in the brief) |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:318:| Grade 3 year-end check (week 29; respond week 30) | 7 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 4 and 5, with one item each from units 2 and 3 (the influence check on a drink label, NHES.S2.5.1; spot the careful choice, NHES.S5.5.1): the I-statement for a character (NHES.S4.5.1), the listening steps on a scene (NHES.S4.5.2), a goal sentence with its reason (NHES.S6.5.1) and an alone-or-together choice (NHES.S6.5.2), how to grow a strength (NHES.S1.5.1). Unit 4's spoken record is carried, not re-heard. Never units 6 or 7. Results go to the grade 4 teacher |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:320:| Grade 4 year-end check (week 29; respond week 30) | 14 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 11 and 12, with one item each from units 8 and 9 (a plan with a check, NHES.S6.5.3; two options and their results, NHES.S5.5.3): how one influence affects two characters (NHES.S2.5.2, NHES.S2.5.3), the source check on a new claim (NHES.S3.5.3), the boundary steps and the refusal steps for a character (NHES.S4.5.4, NHES.S4.5.5), a speak-up plan for a new case (NHES.S8.5.1, NHES.S8.5.2). Unit 12's spoken record is carried, not re-heard. Never units 13 or 14. Results go to the grade 5 teacher |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:340:The SEL 3 to 5 rows lean on health rows (data/progressions/sel.csv cross_subject); each SEL row names its own SEL
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:341:unit, and the SEL frame sets its weeks, so no SEL week is given here. The two courses use one feelings chart (SEL's),
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:342:one calm-down card (SEL's calm-down steps), one I-statement (health's words, grade 3 form "I feel ___ when ___ because
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:343:___. Please ___.") and one upstander plan (SEL's), so a skill is not taught twice in a year.
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:345:| SEL row | Leans on | Unit here | Note, with the if-later line |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:347:| g3-sa-name-feelings (SEL unit 1) | g3-meh-feelings-say | 4 (grade 3 weeks 17 to 21) | SEL's unit 1 likely comes first and teaches the feelings words; health uses them. If SEL comes later, health's day 1 names the feeling with SEL's chart words itself |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:348:| g3-rel-ask-offer-help (SEL unit 4) | g3-s-which-helper | 3 (grade 3 weeks 10 to 14) | Health teaches which helper fits a safety problem; SEL's problem size meter uses the same helpers. If SEL comes first, health retrieves the meter's "big problem: a trusted adult" |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:349:| g3-rel-stand-up (SEL unit 5) | g3-v-bullying | 4 | Health teaches what bullying is; SEL rehearses the upstander plan. If SEL comes later, health's day teaches one upstander line and telling an adult itself |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:350:| g3-sm-set-goal (SEL unit 6) | g3-phw-set-goal | 5 (grade 3 weeks 22 to 24) | Health applies goal setting to a health habit on the safe list; SEL's goal card is for school goals. If SEL comes first, health names its four steps beside the goal-plan |
docs/courses/health-grades-3-to-5/frame.md:351:| g4-sm-set-goal (SEL unit 2) | g4-phw-goal-plan | 8 (grade 4 weeks 1 to 4) | Health comes first in the year by design |
