1:# The Curio Class school calendar
3:One calendar for every course, so a frame, a scope and sequence and a day plan can say "week 14" and mean the same week. The numbers here are positions in a school year, not dates: a school sets its own start, holidays and testing days, and the calendar stays the same.
5:Settled 2026-11-14 (baseline 1 structure findings 4 and 6). The gate is `scripts/frame.py`; `python -X utf8 scripts/frame.py sync` rewrites `data/plans/calendar.csv` and the table at the end of this file, and `python -X utf8 scripts/frame.py calendar` prints the course-level check weeks and any break of the stagger rule.
7:## The words
11:| School day | One of the 160 teaching days of the year, numbered 1 to 160. The calendar has no holidays, testing days or half days: a school lays its own year over these 160. |
17:A frame counts course days. Only the calendar turns them into school days and weeks.
19:**36 weeks and 32.** A real school year is about 36 weeks (about 180 days); this calendar numbers the 160 days a
21:call on the home path named "a 36-week numbered calendar"; whether the home calendar spreads the 160 days over 36
24:## How a course meets
29:| Science 3 to 5 | Four days a week | 30 (120 course days), so the year ends with two weeks free for the other subjects' year-end checks |
30:| Science and social studies K to 2 | Fewer than five days a week, as their frames set | Science K 29, grade 1 29, grade 2 28; social studies K 30, grade 1 31, grade 2 31 (the `WEEKS` table in `scripts/frame.py`), so their year-end checks come before ELA's and math's |
34:A course with D course days spread over W weeks puts course day n on school day `start + ceil(n x 5W / D)`, so its days fall evenly across its weeks. Once a scope and sequence is written, its `data/plans/<course>/days.csv` may give each day a `school_day` column, and that column wins. From 2026-11-14 `scripts/frame.py` builds that course's rows of `data/plans/calendar.csv` from its days.csv (`days_calendar`): each day on its `school_day` where filled and on the even spread otherwise, a unit's or a check's days cut into separate rows wherever another day falls between, so a held day (a look-back taught weeks later, an on-the-day holiday lesson) shows in its own week. A course with no days.csv, or one whose check days do not match its frame's Course-level checks table, is still read from its frame.
36:## Course-level checks across a grade (the stagger rule)
38:A course-level check is a check that sits outside every unit and covers several units (GLOSSARY). Each one is followed by its respond day or days.
40:1. **K to 5, one teacher.** No school week holds the course-level check days of two subjects. A grade's year-end checks fall in four different weeks: science and social studies first (weeks 28 to 30), math in week 31, ELA in week 32.
41:2. **K to 2.** The ELA and math one-to-one checks fall in different weeks and at least five school days apart, because one adult hears every child alone.
42:3. **6 to 8, a teacher per subject.** No school day holds two subjects' mid-year check days. Week 32 is a finals week, as in high school, and may hold every subject's year-end check.
44:5. **The tools, in this order.** (a) A course that meets fewer than five days a week chooses its weeks. (b) Review days split around a check. (c) A check moves to the other side of the unit next to it, and its "What it checks" cell changes with it. A unit never moves to make room for a check.
46:The frame-side moves that bring every K to 8 grade inside the rule are listed per frame in `docs/reviews/baseline-1-structure-fixes-2026-11-14.md`. They were applied on 2026-11-14 (docs/reviews/baseline-1-cleanup-2026-11-14.md), and `frame.py calendar` shows no break. Tool (a) is written as a pin in `PINS` in scripts/frame.py: a course day placed on a chosen school day, with the days before and after it spread evenly. Three courses use one (science grade 2, social studies grade 2, science grade 5), each named in its frame's Days bullet.
48:## Target weeks, K to 8
50:The weeks each course-level check falls in. A scope and sequence places its checks on these weeks.
