75: ## 3. How the standard maps to our courses
76: 
77: Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): one course per grade K to 5, then one course for
78: grades 6 to 8 and one for grades 9 to 12, eight courses. The K to 5 courses each plan one year, so the skills grow
79: every year. The 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 courses spread across their grades, about one session every two weeks, taught
80: alongside other classes so research skills come back each year. Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1).
81: Course ids: `library-k`, `library-1` to `library-5`, `library-6-8`, `library-9-12`, one row each in data/courses.csv
82: in the band-split form of health, PE and arts (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-library-1).
83: 
84: **Ownership** (ours, untested). Each of the 68 ids is owned by exactly one grade, the school year where it is first
85: met at the depth a check can score on its own (not the "with help" level); every other year where a course returns
86: to it carries `~ID` with a one-line note on how that year differs. Grades 6 to 8 and 9 to 12 get a progression row
87: per school year (6, 7, 8; 9, 10, 11, 12) even though each is one course: the progression's grades are the school
88: years, the course is how they are taught. Each row's `assumes` names only the earlier library row it builds on
89: (empty in kindergarten; each school year's row assumes the library row of the year before, so no grade is skipped),
90: since the gate accepts only rows of the same file there; an ELA or social studies row it builds on goes in
91: `cross_subject` (section 5). The gate's library entry in scripts/progression.py uses the standards file, the
92: anchor, ids with no grade (any grade may own one; the one-owner check does the work) and
93: data/progressions/library-excluded.csv for the ids section 4 places elsewhere. Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21): own once, at
94: the school year where the id is first checked alone; `library-6-8` stays one course across grades 6 to 8, with a
95: row per school year.
