CURRENT GRADE 6 NOTES
g6-rel-ask-offer-help Unit 1, Finding my way. The student sizes the problems an invented sixth grader meets (a lost schedule, a stuck locker, a worry that does not go away) on the problem size meter, chooses who to ask among options (a friend, a teacher, the counselor, a message to a family adult), rehearses the ask aloud with a partner playing the chosen helper, and writes why that helper fits. Step up from grade 5: from asking one helper despite embarrassment to choosing the helper that fits the size of the problem. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g6-sa-name-feelings Unit 2, Who am I now?. The grades 3 to 5 1 to 5 strength scale gains the exact word among close ones. The student names and rates an invented character's feelings in a first week of middle school, chooses the most exact word among close ones (nervous, uneasy, dreading) and writes the clue that fits it. Step up from grade 5: from naming mixed feelings to choosing the exact word and its strength. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded.
g6-rel-stand-up Unit 6, Goals and our team. The grades 3 to 5 upstander plan gains "report online", for four options (speak up, support the person afterward, get an adult, report online). The student chooses the safest for an invented case of a student mocked in a team chat, rehearses the chosen words with a partner while the teacher or a card plays the one posting the mockery (the I-statement frame for a speak-up, the plain facts for a report), and writes why. Step up from grade 5: from one planned speak-up to a choice among upstander options. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course.

